1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
We were going over preference heterogeneity.

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:04,080
I was talking a bit about my paper

3
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,400
on personality in economics.

4
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:08,440
So does anyone here have a personality?

5
00:00:08,440 --> 00:00:10,440
All right.

6
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:13,240
There was a period in the 60s when personality psychology

7
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:15,200
fell out of favor in psychology.

8
00:00:15,200 --> 00:00:18,960
And there was a common view that personality was a myth.

9
00:00:18,960 --> 00:00:20,360
Based upon some experiments where

10
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:22,400
it was very hard to predict people's behavior.

11
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:24,600
And then finally people noticed that there was just

12
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:25,980
a lot of randomness if you just take

13
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:27,980
a larger number of observations and average them

14
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:31,000
and as you'd expect, there were in fact personalities

15
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,440
that people have.

16
00:00:32,440 --> 00:00:36,040
It is kind of amazing to me that someone would ever doubt that.

17
00:00:36,040 --> 00:00:39,600
So there are many things that personality psychology

18
00:00:39,600 --> 00:00:41,680
sheds some light on, or so I argue,

19
00:00:41,680 --> 00:00:43,840
sheds a lot of light on occupational choice.

20
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:47,120
So whatever stereotypes you have about what personalities

21
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:51,200
tend to be in what occupations are very likely to be true.

22
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,320
So salesmen are extroverts.

23
00:00:53,320 --> 00:00:54,680
Librarians are introverts.

24
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:55,840
Very true.

25
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:58,560
You've got a ranking about 500 occupations,

26
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:01,200
salesmen will be very near the top.

27
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:05,080
Librarians will be very near the bottom for extroversion.

28
00:01:05,080 --> 00:01:07,800
Then you have things like the trait of agreeableness,

29
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:09,960
where you'll see that people involved in say,

30
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:13,520
preschool education tend to be highly agreeable people.

31
00:01:13,520 --> 00:01:14,760
Like they're nice.

32
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:16,440
On the other hand, economists tend

33
00:01:16,440 --> 00:01:18,920
to have very low agreeableness.

34
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:23,080
We're nice in our own way, but not considered nice

35
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:26,120
by a great many people.

36
00:01:26,120 --> 00:01:29,520
There's also Alan Blander's book, Hardhead Soft Hearts,

37
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:31,040
which is interesting.

38
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,840
So Hardhead Soft Hearts.

39
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:36,000
The book is interesting because he is playing off

40
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:37,720
a stereotype, which is that either you've

41
00:01:37,720 --> 00:01:39,440
got a hard head and a hard heart,

42
00:01:39,440 --> 00:01:41,640
or you've got a soft head and a soft heart.

43
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:43,480
And what personality psychology says

44
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:45,640
is that there is, in fact, a large gender

45
00:01:45,640 --> 00:01:47,600
to gender correlation.

46
00:01:47,600 --> 00:01:50,040
So people who think logically also just

47
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:51,880
tend to be very cold.

48
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:54,040
On the other hand, people who care about people

49
00:01:54,040 --> 00:01:56,640
don't care very much about the best way to do it.

50
00:01:56,640 --> 00:01:59,240
Which is a bad word to live in, but that's our world.

51
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:02,400
So of course, there's no rule that you can't be both.

52
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:08,440
But on average, this is, in fact, what we see in the data.

53
00:02:08,440 --> 00:02:10,480
And it might also explain why it is

54
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:12,720
that people don't trust or people are overly logical,

55
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:15,240
because they seem like they don't care.

56
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:19,040
Would you want Mr. Spock to be a preschool teacher?

57
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:23,000
Well, on one hand, he's very accurate in assessing facts.

58
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:25,440
But on the other hand, if a child died,

59
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:29,120
you say, well, Captain, 10,000 children died today.

60
00:02:29,120 --> 00:02:31,840
Why is this one any different from any other?

61
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:33,640
That kind of reasoning would not be

62
00:02:33,640 --> 00:02:36,280
someone you would probably want to have as a child care

63
00:02:36,280 --> 00:02:38,560
provider, I think.

64
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:41,800
And then things like writers tend

65
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:43,200
to be high in openness, policemen

66
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:45,360
tend to be low in openness.

67
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:46,920
Let's see, four.

68
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:49,200
And then another one is the trait that

69
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:52,520
is called conscientiousness is a general predictor

70
00:02:52,520 --> 00:02:54,720
of good job performance.

71
00:02:54,720 --> 00:02:57,160
If you've ever taken the Myers-Briggs test,

72
00:02:57,160 --> 00:02:59,560
there's the trait of judging versus perceiving.

73
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:02,960
This is essentially trying to cover up some ugly truths

74
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:04,720
with some misleading names.

75
00:03:04,720 --> 00:03:06,680
Judging equals conscientious.

76
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:09,520
Perceiving equals not conscientious.

77
00:03:09,520 --> 00:03:12,600
What jobs do you want the not conscientious people for?

78
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:14,800
Really, you would just rather avoid them if you could.

79
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:15,880
Of course, they're fantastic friends.

80
00:03:15,880 --> 00:03:18,520
I love my unconscious people that I know.

81
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:21,600
In fact, conscientious people can be quite boring.

82
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:23,560
If you have concert tickets for the last minute,

83
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:24,920
don't call your conscientious friends.

84
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:27,180
They will say they have to work or they have to study.

85
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:28,040
They can't come.

86
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:31,040
But if you're avoiding people, then you probably

87
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:33,400
want the people who don't jump at the opportunity

88
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:37,440
to see a concert on a weeknight at the last minute.

89
00:03:37,440 --> 00:03:39,080
Then there's also been work on things

90
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:41,400
like the personalities of criminals.

91
00:03:41,400 --> 00:03:45,520
And these are some very obvious yet interesting results.

92
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:48,960
So criminals tend to have very low conscientiousness

93
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:51,040
and very low agreeableness.

94
00:03:51,040 --> 00:03:55,120
So they are mean, lazy, defiant people who don't follow rules.

95
00:03:57,680 --> 00:03:58,640
Not so surprising.

96
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:02,320
But in a way, what really was surprising

97
00:04:02,320 --> 00:04:04,760
is that you can go and take measures of these traits

98
00:04:04,760 --> 00:04:09,800
from fairly young ages and then predict outcomes decades later.

99
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:14,400
So there's a paper that looks at personality tests that are,

100
00:04:14,400 --> 00:04:15,760
these are paper and pencil tests.

101
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:19,040
You just fill out a form that you take around the age of 18.

102
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:21,640
And then they see what you can predict when people are 30

103
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:22,960
or 40 or 50.

104
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:24,400
And you can predict a lot of stuff.

105
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:26,040
You can predict divorce.

106
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:29,160
Who are the people who get divorced?

107
00:04:29,160 --> 00:04:29,720
Let's see.

108
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:31,280
Yeah, it looks like you've got a few.

109
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:34,320
Oh, I'm going to guess people who aren't conscientious.

110
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:36,160
Yes, low conscientious people get a little.

111
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:38,660
The conscientious people tend to have higher divorce rates,

112
00:04:38,660 --> 00:04:39,640
noticeably so.

113
00:04:39,640 --> 00:04:42,760
Right people follow the rules and expectations

114
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:45,840
and don't consider everything a special, exceptional circumstance

115
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:47,840
that doesn't, so the rules don't apply to them.

116
00:04:47,840 --> 00:04:49,280
They tend to stay married more.

117
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:51,480
Agreeable people tend to stay married more.

118
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:53,080
People who are just nicer.

119
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:54,680
And also people who are less neurotic.

120
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:58,520
People who just have fewer negative emotions,

121
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:00,800
they also tend to stay married longer.

122
00:05:00,800 --> 00:05:02,520
You can use it to correct suicide.

123
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:05,440
So there's lots of things you can do with these tests.

124
00:05:05,440 --> 00:05:06,600
Can they explain everything?

125
00:05:06,600 --> 00:05:07,400
No.

126
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:10,960
Let's see, what's another field that can't explain everything?

127
00:05:10,960 --> 00:05:11,480
Which one?

128
00:05:11,480 --> 00:05:12,520
Let's see.

129
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:14,880
We may have some shared interest in this field.

130
00:05:14,880 --> 00:05:15,560
Economics.

131
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:18,760
Economics, yes, also can't explain everything.

132
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:19,320
OK.

133
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:21,400
So anyway, so preferences only explain everything,

134
00:05:21,400 --> 00:05:23,440
and the preferences are not equally measured.

135
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:26,760
Now, since I wrote this piece, one Nobel laureate in economics

136
00:05:26,760 --> 00:05:28,360
has gotten very interesting personality,

137
00:05:28,360 --> 00:05:30,160
that's James Heckman.

138
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:32,120
And he's written many papers, co-authored

139
00:05:32,120 --> 00:05:33,720
with lots of other people.

140
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:37,760
I think it's possible that James Heckman has single-handedly,

141
00:05:37,760 --> 00:05:39,240
well, that's the only way to do it,

142
00:05:39,240 --> 00:05:41,960
but papers with James Heckman as one of the authors

143
00:05:41,960 --> 00:05:44,480
probably constitute 80% of all papers in economics

144
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:45,400
on the subject.

145
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:47,120
But he's been doing very well publishing

146
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:49,400
on this theme, which paves the way for others.

147
00:05:49,400 --> 00:05:51,840
He never cites me, but of course, what matters

148
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:53,080
is that the ideas live.

149
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:54,840
It doesn't matter if I get credit, right?

150
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:00,040
So questions about personality.

151
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:01,200
Plenty more to do here.

152
00:06:01,200 --> 00:06:03,120
Economists still don't have that much interest

153
00:06:03,120 --> 00:06:04,360
in the general topic.

154
00:06:04,360 --> 00:06:05,720
One thing that's closely related,

155
00:06:05,720 --> 00:06:09,440
so I was doing a bunch of erruptions on crime.

156
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:12,520
So in the National Longitudinal Study of Youth,

157
00:06:12,520 --> 00:06:14,280
they actually have administrative data

158
00:06:14,280 --> 00:06:19,120
on whether you were in prison when they interviewed you.

159
00:06:19,120 --> 00:06:20,640
And they track people down.

160
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:23,120
So they hunt you down, and then if they find you in prison,

161
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:25,840
they go and code you was in prison when interviewed.

162
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:27,520
So we've got actual administrative data

163
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:28,840
on when you're in prison.

164
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:32,160
And then they also had a bunch of self-reported information

165
00:06:32,160 --> 00:06:36,600
about your behavior in junior high and high school.

166
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:38,880
Key thing, the behavior in junior high and high school

167
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:41,840
was reported when you were in junior high or high school.

168
00:06:41,840 --> 00:06:43,960
And then decades later, we come back

169
00:06:43,960 --> 00:06:46,280
to see whether we can predict how many years you spent

170
00:06:46,280 --> 00:06:49,560
in jail based upon your behavior as a child

171
00:06:49,560 --> 00:06:51,080
or as an adolescent.

172
00:06:51,080 --> 00:06:53,960
And it turns out those behavioral measures

173
00:06:53,960 --> 00:06:57,520
are the best predictors that exist of adult criminality.

174
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:02,840
Still hardly perfect, but predictors like,

175
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:05,920
did you ever use marijuana?

176
00:07:05,920 --> 00:07:08,040
When did you start using it?

177
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:09,000
Have you ever had sex?

178
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:10,080
When did you start having sex?

179
00:07:10,080 --> 00:07:11,040
Have you run away from home?

180
00:07:11,040 --> 00:07:12,320
Why did you run away from home?

181
00:07:12,320 --> 00:07:13,560
Have you ever been suspended from school?

182
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,260
When were you first suspended at school?

183
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:19,080
We put all this stuff in of just general defiant behavior,

184
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:22,840
which you may say the behavior's great, but I'm just a messenger.

185
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:24,840
This behavior predicts other stuff.

186
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:26,200
Doesn't mean it causes it.

187
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:28,000
Again, once you take personality seriously,

188
00:07:28,000 --> 00:07:30,600
the obvious story is there are some people who

189
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:34,880
are just defiant people who don't want to follow rules.

190
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:37,240
And they tend to break a lot of minor rules

191
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:38,360
when they're in adolescence.

192
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:40,880
And then in adulthood, they tend to break more serious rules

193
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:47,280
and then end up in prison, sometimes for a long time.

194
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:50,360
All right, so questions about that?

195
00:07:50,360 --> 00:07:50,860
Yes?

196
00:07:50,860 --> 00:07:52,820
Is there any research about cultural influences

197
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:53,480
on personality?

198
00:07:53,480 --> 00:07:56,760
Because I hear the pieces a lot about the Scotch-Irish people

199
00:07:56,760 --> 00:07:58,920
who live in Kentucky, West Virginia,

200
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:01,080
just fighting and being defiant and things like that.

201
00:08:01,080 --> 00:08:01,640
Right, right.

202
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:03,240
So there's a very well-known paper

203
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:05,240
by two of the best-known personality researchers

204
00:08:05,240 --> 00:08:08,000
who claim that these cultural stereotypes are

205
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:11,560
one of the few stereotypes about personality that aren't true.

206
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:15,400
Although, here's a key thing.

207
00:08:15,400 --> 00:08:16,320
These are all sub-eo.

208
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:18,560
These personality measures are always self-reported.

209
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:23,640
So if you are reporting relative to what your culture is like,

210
00:08:23,640 --> 00:08:27,360
then this will actually mask all effects,

211
00:08:27,360 --> 00:08:28,600
which could still be there.

212
00:08:28,600 --> 00:08:30,560
So sort of the amazing thing is that usually it

213
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:33,120
works really well, despite the tendency of people

214
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:35,920
to rate themselves relative to some subgroup.

215
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:38,880
For example, are stereotypes about gender and personality

216
00:08:38,880 --> 00:08:41,360
work, even though it's probably reasonable to think

217
00:08:41,360 --> 00:08:43,520
that men are rating themselves kind of compared

218
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:45,360
to other male personalities, women

219
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:46,980
are kind of rating themselves compared

220
00:08:46,980 --> 00:08:48,280
to other female personalities.

221
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:51,000
So whatever group differences you see are probably bigger,

222
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:54,600
and probably noticeably bigger, than what's reported.

223
00:08:54,600 --> 00:08:59,200
Because if people do report themselves relative to a base,

224
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:01,840
and of course, this is also true for things like happiness.

225
00:09:01,840 --> 00:09:05,360
So for example, you see East Asians

226
00:09:05,360 --> 00:09:08,080
tend to report low happiness.

227
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:10,880
Now if, especially, this is clear,

228
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,320
it's actually in East Asia.

229
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:15,480
But anyway, so a Japanese report being unusually unhappy,

230
00:09:15,480 --> 00:09:17,680
considering how rich they are.

231
00:09:17,680 --> 00:09:21,040
Now if people in Japan measure their happiness compared

232
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:23,520
to other Japanese people, and if Japanese people in general

233
00:09:23,520 --> 00:09:26,520
aren't very happy, then the fact that we

234
00:09:26,520 --> 00:09:28,920
see this difference in happiness might say they're really

235
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:30,080
unhappy.

236
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:32,200
And Latin America is exactly the opposite.

237
00:09:32,200 --> 00:09:34,120
Latin Americans say they're really happy,

238
00:09:34,120 --> 00:09:35,880
especially if you go and adjust for income.

239
00:09:35,880 --> 00:09:38,720
Then wow, they're so happy down there, despite everything.

240
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:40,960
And then if there's some kind of a cultural adjustment

241
00:09:40,960 --> 00:09:42,420
where you look around and you see,

242
00:09:42,420 --> 00:09:44,520
well, I'm only smiling half the time,

243
00:09:44,520 --> 00:09:47,240
normal people smile 3 quarters of the time.

244
00:09:47,240 --> 00:09:49,080
Then you might go and say, I'm pretty happy,

245
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:52,880
when if someone from that person in Latin America were in Japan,

246
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:54,680
they might consider themselves super happy.

247
00:09:54,680 --> 00:09:55,680
All right.

248
00:09:55,680 --> 00:09:57,960
So moving on then.

249
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:00,480
But plenty more to do on this stuff, certainly.

250
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:04,160
And Heckman has been the icebreaker for future research

251
00:10:04,160 --> 00:10:05,960
in a way that I have not been.

252
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:07,960
So you can follow in Heckman's wake

253
00:10:07,960 --> 00:10:09,760
and do it better than he did.

254
00:10:09,760 --> 00:10:13,760
And honestly, in some ways, it wouldn't be that hard.

255
00:10:13,760 --> 00:10:15,360
So I have looked at some of his data.

256
00:10:15,360 --> 00:10:17,520
And I'm just going to say, basically,

257
00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:19,760
he's done a lot of work with the National Longitudinal

258
00:10:19,760 --> 00:10:20,360
Study of Youth.

259
00:10:20,360 --> 00:10:22,000
And he looked at two particular areas.

260
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,680
He did the National Longitudinal Study of Youth, and he looked

261
00:10:24,680 --> 00:10:27,160
at two particular variables, which, as far as I've seen,

262
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:29,520
are some of the least predictive variables in personality

263
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:30,320
that we're seeing.

264
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:31,960
And he does some very fancy techniques

265
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:34,440
to sort of squeeze a little bit of prediction

266
00:10:34,440 --> 00:10:36,640
out of some highly unpredictable variables.

267
00:10:36,640 --> 00:10:39,200
But there's much better data sets out there that say more.

268
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:42,480
And even in the National Longitudinal Study of Youth,

269
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:44,880
we have all these adolescent behavior measures,

270
00:10:44,880 --> 00:10:48,960
which you can think of as being kind of like personality.

271
00:10:48,960 --> 00:10:50,480
All right.

272
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:51,600
So moving on.

273
00:10:51,600 --> 00:10:52,680
Expect-utility anomalies.

274
00:10:52,680 --> 00:10:54,240
So remember, expect-utility theory

275
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:57,960
puts some definite restrictions on choice under uncertainty.

276
00:10:57,960 --> 00:11:00,360
Many of these have been experimentally falsified.

277
00:11:00,360 --> 00:11:02,560
So even though the theory says that people

278
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:04,320
act in a certain way under uncertainty,

279
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:07,360
we put their actual tests, and they confidently do not.

280
00:11:07,360 --> 00:11:10,440
So one of them is people seem to be risk averse over utility

281
00:11:10,440 --> 00:11:11,560
rather than wealth.

282
00:11:11,560 --> 00:11:13,840
So remember the way that we actually

283
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:16,800
model risk aversion in the expect-utility framework.

284
00:11:16,800 --> 00:11:21,000
We do something like utility equals square root of wealth.

285
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:24,120
Remember, that indicates what kind of a utility function?

286
00:11:24,120 --> 00:11:26,160
Risk averse, risk neutral, risk preferring?

287
00:11:26,160 --> 00:11:26,680
Risk averse.

288
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:27,180
Risk averse.

289
00:11:27,180 --> 00:11:29,160
It's a risk averse utility function, right?

290
00:11:29,160 --> 00:11:32,440
So the way that expect-utility theory thinks about risk

291
00:11:32,440 --> 00:11:35,760
aversion is that you're risk averse over your wealth states.

292
00:11:35,760 --> 00:11:39,520
But in experiments, you can ask people things like, well,

293
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:42,000
suppose you could either have a happiness of 10.

294
00:11:42,000 --> 00:11:43,840
I'm trying to get people to do it correctly.

295
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:47,480
You either have a happiness of 10 with a 70% chance,

296
00:11:47,480 --> 00:11:50,400
or happiness of 5, but otherwise 0.

297
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:51,960
Or you have a happiness of 5 for sure.

298
00:11:51,960 --> 00:11:53,200
Which one would we take?

299
00:11:53,200 --> 00:12:02,520
Well, we'd do it 0.7 times 10 plus 0.3 times 0 equals 0.7.

300
00:12:02,520 --> 00:12:06,520
And then 1 times 0.5, that could be your happiness, equals 0.5.

301
00:12:06,520 --> 00:12:09,280
So under expect-utility theory, every person

302
00:12:09,280 --> 00:12:11,960
would choose a 70% chance of being super happy

303
00:12:11,960 --> 00:12:14,840
with a 30% chance of being super miserable.

304
00:12:14,840 --> 00:12:17,920
But in practice, people seem to have risk aversion over that,

305
00:12:17,920 --> 00:12:19,760
too.

306
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:21,600
And even very local risk aversion,

307
00:12:21,600 --> 00:12:23,600
where people want to be sure that they'll

308
00:12:23,600 --> 00:12:26,520
be pretty happy each day.

309
00:12:26,520 --> 00:12:28,400
Well, what if on this one day, you

310
00:12:28,400 --> 00:12:30,240
had a 70% chance of having a great day,

311
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:31,840
but a 30% chance of having a bad day,

312
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:34,320
or we could have a mediocre day for sure?

313
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:36,640
A lot of people will say I have a mediocre day for sure,

314
00:12:36,640 --> 00:12:38,680
even if based on their own rankings,

315
00:12:38,680 --> 00:12:40,760
it seems like their average would be higher

316
00:12:40,760 --> 00:12:41,760
if they took the gamble.

317
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:46,720
All right, so people seem risk averse over utility,

318
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:47,680
not just wealth.

319
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:49,440
People often seem risk averse relative

320
00:12:49,440 --> 00:12:51,240
to a reference point.

321
00:12:51,240 --> 00:12:53,160
Risk averse relative to a reference point.

322
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:58,760
So for example, suppose that Bill Gates plays a low stakes

323
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:01,400
game of poker.

324
00:13:01,400 --> 00:13:04,160
There's a lot of experimental evidence that at least most

325
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:06,400
people, maybe not Bill Gates, we don't know about him.

326
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:09,160
He has better things to do than me in his experiments.

327
00:13:09,160 --> 00:13:12,640
But most people, even though they're billionaires,

328
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:14,440
will do things like betting cautiously

329
00:13:14,440 --> 00:13:18,200
to avoid losing money, which in expect-utility theory

330
00:13:18,200 --> 00:13:20,360
makes almost no sense, especially

331
00:13:20,360 --> 00:13:24,280
if that same very rich person bets a billion dollars every day

332
00:13:24,280 --> 00:13:27,040
in the stock market and is losing tens of millions

333
00:13:27,040 --> 00:13:29,120
or gaining tens of millions of dollars every day

334
00:13:29,120 --> 00:13:30,360
without even caring.

335
00:13:30,360 --> 00:13:32,840
But then in this game of poker, it's like, oh my god,

336
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:33,440
I can't lose.

337
00:13:33,440 --> 00:13:35,240
I can't lose this game of poker where you're

338
00:13:35,240 --> 00:13:37,600
paying for a dollar a hand.

339
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:40,880
And this resonates a lot with me because the older I get,

340
00:13:40,880 --> 00:13:42,880
the more people I know who have plenty of money,

341
00:13:42,880 --> 00:13:45,240
and yet I still see these people getting really worried

342
00:13:45,240 --> 00:13:48,160
about getting a parking ticket for $20 and things like that.

343
00:13:48,160 --> 00:13:51,920
Like, you gamble with money, it was sums of money way bigger

344
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:52,920
than this all the time.

345
00:13:52,920 --> 00:13:54,300
But it's like, well, I just don't

346
00:13:54,300 --> 00:13:57,040
want to have to be out of pocket on this ticket.

347
00:13:57,040 --> 00:14:00,240
But you're spending $100 worth of time

348
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:02,800
in order to avoid a ticket that, in the worst case scenario,

349
00:14:02,800 --> 00:14:04,480
might well cost you half that.

350
00:14:04,480 --> 00:14:07,120
It's like, well, yeah, but I don't

351
00:14:07,120 --> 00:14:09,920
want to be out of pocket, this mentality.

352
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:12,840
And again, what does even that phrase, out of pocket, mean?

353
00:14:12,840 --> 00:14:16,800
It's not really well-defined in normal economic terms, right?

354
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:19,160
I don't want to be out of pocket.

355
00:14:19,160 --> 00:14:20,240
What does that mean?

356
00:14:20,240 --> 00:14:22,640
Well, out of pocket means relative to a reference point,

357
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:24,080
where I have a certain amount of starting money,

358
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:25,960
and it would just be terrible if I ended up

359
00:14:25,960 --> 00:14:30,160
with any amount of money less than that.

360
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:31,080
Now, another thing.

361
00:14:31,080 --> 00:14:32,640
So remember how I was telling you

362
00:14:32,640 --> 00:14:36,360
that in expense-utility theory, the way that things work

363
00:14:36,360 --> 00:14:39,600
is you go and you take probabilities,

364
00:14:39,600 --> 00:14:41,040
and you multiply them by outcomes.

365
00:14:41,040 --> 00:14:44,320
So probability times utility of the outcome.

366
00:14:44,320 --> 00:14:46,960
And what that means is that the difference

367
00:14:46,960 --> 00:14:50,840
between a 2% probability and a 1% probability

368
00:14:50,840 --> 00:14:53,680
is no different than a 1% probability and a 0%

369
00:14:53,680 --> 00:14:55,600
probability.

370
00:14:55,600 --> 00:14:58,320
In the real world, it often doesn't work this way.

371
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:00,840
In the real world, people pay a lot more

372
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:05,480
to go from 1% to 0% than they do to go from 2% to 1%.

373
00:15:05,480 --> 00:15:08,020
And there's plenty of people that will pay basically nothing

374
00:15:08,020 --> 00:15:13,000
to go from 2% to 1%, but will pay a ton to go from 1 to 0.

375
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:14,960
There's contraband expense-utility theory.

376
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:17,760
So again, think about if your odds of dying of cancer

377
00:15:17,760 --> 00:15:19,920
were $2, in the next 10 years, we're 2%.

378
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:23,120
How much would you pay to bring it down to 1?

379
00:15:23,120 --> 00:15:24,440
I don't know.

380
00:15:24,440 --> 00:15:25,760
$100, $100.

381
00:15:25,760 --> 00:15:27,800
But then, suppose you got your source at 1%.

382
00:15:27,800 --> 00:15:29,520
You could bring it down to nothing.

383
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:31,000
That's when people might actually

384
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:34,680
max out their credit card to go and get rid of it.

385
00:15:34,680 --> 00:15:37,320
And would it matter if you spent time

386
00:15:37,320 --> 00:15:40,680
going into all the gruesome details of how bad cancer is?

387
00:15:40,680 --> 00:15:42,600
I think it would matter a lot more for that one

388
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:45,600
than for that one, if you give them all the horrors of it.

389
00:15:45,600 --> 00:15:47,240
But then they'll say, well, 2% to 1%,

390
00:15:47,240 --> 00:15:49,280
but I could still have all those horrible things,

391
00:15:49,280 --> 00:15:51,560
so what's the point of paying all that money?

392
00:15:51,560 --> 00:15:54,920
Whereas 1% to 0%, well, then none of that could happen to me,

393
00:15:54,920 --> 00:15:56,760
so it's worth a lot of money.

394
00:15:56,760 --> 00:15:59,280
But in each case, you're reducing a horrible thing

395
00:15:59,280 --> 00:16:01,240
by 1 percentage point.

396
00:16:01,240 --> 00:16:04,320
So in terms of the logic-expense-utility theory,

397
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:06,720
those two things would be equally valuable to you,

398
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:10,120
but that's not how people act.

399
00:16:10,120 --> 00:16:14,080
And if you just think about the way that public policy makes

400
00:16:14,080 --> 00:16:16,960
these ridiculous efforts to go from a really small probability

401
00:16:16,960 --> 00:16:19,120
down to zero, which they can't even do,

402
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:21,440
but they can pretend to do so.

403
00:16:21,440 --> 00:16:23,480
So worry about getting the risk of terrorism

404
00:16:23,480 --> 00:16:26,320
down from its incredibly small level to zero.

405
00:16:26,320 --> 00:16:32,080
How many trillions are spent in this futile effort?

406
00:16:32,080 --> 00:16:35,120
Well, many trillions, when you add it all up over the years.

407
00:16:35,120 --> 00:16:37,920
All right.

408
00:16:37,920 --> 00:16:40,200
So and then, last one.

409
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:41,800
So the LA paradox, this is one that you

410
00:16:41,800 --> 00:16:45,160
get to do to prove on your homework.

411
00:16:45,160 --> 00:16:48,160
But I'll just go and give you the original numbers

412
00:16:48,160 --> 00:16:51,120
and probabilities to give you an idea about what

413
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:52,920
you're going to be proving.

414
00:16:52,920 --> 00:16:55,440
All right, so associated with Nobel laureate,

415
00:16:55,440 --> 00:16:57,640
I think actually Creole found it.

416
00:16:57,640 --> 00:17:00,160
I think he did actually come up with these exact numbers

417
00:17:00,160 --> 00:17:01,040
as well, the paradox.

418
00:17:01,040 --> 00:17:03,800
Maurice LA won the Nobel Prize, I think actually partly

419
00:17:03,800 --> 00:17:05,360
for this.

420
00:17:05,360 --> 00:17:08,240
So imagine you got the following choices of gambles.

421
00:17:08,240 --> 00:17:14,400
So you've got 27,500 with probability 0.33.

422
00:17:14,400 --> 00:17:20,200
You've got 24,000 with probability 0.66.

423
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:25,000
And then you've got zero with probability 0.01.

424
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:28,000
So that's the first possible gamble.

425
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:35,400
And then second possible gamble is 24,000 probability 1.

426
00:17:35,400 --> 00:17:38,920
So you have to choose one of these two gambles.

427
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:42,200
So which one do you think a large majority of people pick?

428
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:43,440
24,000.

429
00:17:43,440 --> 00:17:44,760
Yeah, 24,000.

430
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:47,080
That is the popular pick.

431
00:17:47,080 --> 00:17:50,440
I think at least 2 to 1.

432
00:17:50,440 --> 00:17:52,400
Then we've got another possibility.

433
00:17:52,400 --> 00:17:58,440
Suppose we have 27,500 with probability 0.33.

434
00:17:58,440 --> 00:18:04,160
We've got $0 with probability 0.67.

435
00:18:04,160 --> 00:18:07,320
Let's see, that's gamble one.

436
00:18:07,320 --> 00:18:14,640
And then gamble two is 24,000 with probability 0.34.

437
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:19,040
And then, let's see, 0.34.

438
00:18:19,040 --> 00:18:21,120
And then, let's see, where were we?

439
00:18:21,120 --> 00:18:23,520
Hold on.

440
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:25,880
Yeah, 24,000 with probability 0.34.

441
00:18:25,880 --> 00:18:30,400
And then 0 with probability 0.66.

442
00:18:30,400 --> 00:18:32,360
So that's your other possibility.

443
00:18:32,360 --> 00:18:34,800
And then you're choosing between these two ones.

444
00:18:34,800 --> 00:18:37,680
Now, for these two ones, which one does a large majority

445
00:18:37,680 --> 00:18:38,320
people pick?

446
00:18:38,320 --> 00:18:39,000
First one.

447
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,000
Yeah, the first one.

448
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:42,560
Now, if you're paying attention, it

449
00:18:42,560 --> 00:18:46,240
sure seems like this has a lot to do with this thing here,

450
00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:49,680
where people are really valuing going down from a 1% chance

451
00:18:49,680 --> 00:18:51,520
to a 0% chance of something.

452
00:18:51,520 --> 00:18:57,600
But they don't care very much if they go from a 34% versus a 33%

453
00:18:57,600 --> 00:18:58,800
chance of something.

454
00:18:58,800 --> 00:18:59,880
And that's right.

455
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:01,560
Now, using expected utility theory,

456
00:19:01,560 --> 00:19:03,360
and you will do this on homework,

457
00:19:03,360 --> 00:19:07,640
you can prove that if expected utility theory were true,

458
00:19:07,640 --> 00:19:10,960
everybody would either do, let's see,

459
00:19:10,960 --> 00:19:14,280
everyone would either do a second,

460
00:19:14,280 --> 00:19:16,720
everyone would either want both of these or both of these.

461
00:19:16,720 --> 00:19:20,280
And no one would do this one and that one.

462
00:19:20,280 --> 00:19:22,920
But in fact, about a third of people

463
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:28,120
want to do this one and that one, which is weird.

464
00:19:28,120 --> 00:19:30,520
Or just shows the expected utility theory is wrong.

465
00:19:30,520 --> 00:19:32,680
And again, intuitively, I think you can pretty well

466
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:36,800
see what's going on, where there's a 1% chance of nothing?

467
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:38,000
Oh my god, that's terrible.

468
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:39,620
I've got to get rid of that 1% chance.

469
00:19:39,620 --> 00:19:40,760
That's so terrible.

470
00:19:40,760 --> 00:19:42,720
So OK, fine, I'll just 24,000.

471
00:19:42,720 --> 00:19:44,600
That's pretty much as good as 2,700.

472
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:45,520
OK, good.

473
00:19:45,520 --> 00:19:48,360
It's absolutely sure everything's going to be good.

474
00:19:48,360 --> 00:19:48,920
All right.

475
00:19:48,920 --> 00:19:50,920
Now, over here, this one is like, oh my god.

476
00:19:50,920 --> 00:19:54,200
Well, I'm probably going to get nothing either way, right?

477
00:19:54,200 --> 00:19:56,560
But this one, I just give a yi-yi-yi-yi-yi-yi-yi-yi-yi-yi.

478
00:19:56,560 --> 00:20:16,520
So probably nothing either way, but just a 1% chance more.


1
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,240
of getting nothing and then I can get a way better price.

2
00:20:04,120 --> 00:20:06,880
So you'll get to prove this yourself,

3
00:20:06,920 --> 00:20:08,540
but that is the LA paradox.

4
00:20:10,160 --> 00:20:10,760
All right, questions?

5
00:20:11,380 --> 00:20:11,560
Yes?

6
00:20:11,840 --> 00:20:14,240
When you say choose, I mean, is it,

7
00:20:14,800 --> 00:20:16,520
in what sense is it choose like this?

8
00:20:16,520 --> 00:20:18,520
So normally these are hypothetical responses

9
00:20:18,520 --> 00:20:20,000
where they just say if you have a choice of these two,

10
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:21,880
which one do you want, choice of these two, which one do you want.

11
00:20:22,120 --> 00:20:24,300
It is used literature, so I don't know

12
00:20:24,300 --> 00:20:26,900
if any actual experiment where they're giving people 24,000

13
00:20:26,900 --> 00:20:28,640
or $27,500.

14
00:20:28,640 --> 00:20:30,060
So it seems like you could, I mean,

15
00:20:30,060 --> 00:20:32,260
you do like all the experimental stuff.

16
00:20:32,260 --> 00:20:34,100
If you took a few zeros off the end of it,

17
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:35,900
there would be a possibility to see where.

18
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:37,520
Yeah, so, I mean, like,

19
00:20:37,800 --> 00:20:39,700
what's actually done experimentally is taking zeros

20
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:40,700
off and then redoing it.

21
00:20:41,080 --> 00:20:42,560
And, you know, like, you do get this,

22
00:20:42,560 --> 00:20:44,000
you do get the LA paradox.

23
00:20:44,580 --> 00:20:47,000
I mean, like, in the course of the game, I mean,

24
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:48,840
it wouldn't surprise me if you could bring that,

25
00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:52,380
bring this down to 27 and a half cents, 24 cents,

26
00:20:52,720 --> 00:20:55,720
and still if people had two hours to play this game,

27
00:20:55,720 --> 00:20:58,200
they'd be like, oh my God, I'm only with nothing?

28
00:20:58,200 --> 00:20:59,620
Instead of 27 and a half cents?

29
00:20:59,620 --> 00:21:04,020
So, I'm someone who plays a lot of games,

30
00:21:04,020 --> 00:21:07,160
and I see people getting all worked up over losing a game

31
00:21:07,160 --> 00:21:08,620
where there's no stakes at all.

32
00:21:10,160 --> 00:21:12,100
I can't lose, can't lose.

33
00:21:12,360 --> 00:21:14,220
I've seen people actually pay real money

34
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:17,020
to save their position in a game where there's no money involved.

35
00:21:17,720 --> 00:21:20,420
It's like, I will pay you to go and move your unit there.

36
00:21:20,900 --> 00:21:21,820
It's like 20 bucks.

37
00:21:23,280 --> 00:21:24,560
Hmm. All right.

38
00:21:24,560 --> 00:21:28,500
Okay. So, questions, questions?

