like a bombshell and nobody cared.

43
00:21:33,780 --> 00:21:37,100
What he did present it, is it actually used anywhere?

44
00:21:37,880 --> 00:21:40,800
Well, so, in risk analysis, like in the real,

45
00:21:40,800 --> 00:21:42,640
when you're looking at how people assess risks,

46
00:21:43,100 --> 00:21:44,900
then I don't know if they actually,

47
00:21:44,900 --> 00:21:47,580
if they actually cite a lay, but the general point,

48
00:21:47,580 --> 00:21:49,960
the people seem to put a lot of value in getting rid

49
00:21:49,960 --> 00:21:51,120
of really tiny risks.

50
00:21:51,120 --> 00:21:53,820
And also just the way that the rhetoric

51
00:21:53,820 --> 00:21:55,880
of eliminating a risk appeals to people so much.

52
00:21:56,200 --> 00:21:57,420
The way that you sell a warranty.

53
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,500
You say, look, you will have no danger at all.

54
00:22:00,500 --> 00:22:01,280
Everything will be like,

55
00:22:01,280 --> 00:22:03,640
you are guaranteed to have no liability.

56
00:22:03,640 --> 00:22:05,860
It's like, well, what if you go out of business?

57
00:22:06,320 --> 00:22:07,740
Well, then I guess you have a liability.

58
00:22:07,960 --> 00:22:09,480
So, it's not really guaranteed, is it?

59
00:22:09,480 --> 00:22:11,340
Well, it's guaranteed as long as you don't get a business.

60
00:22:11,540 --> 00:22:13,240
It also means it's not really guaranteed.

61
00:22:13,540 --> 00:22:15,880
So, there's, but the way that people try to frame things

62
00:22:15,880 --> 00:22:18,180
in terms of you will have no possibility of anything wrong.

63
00:22:18,180 --> 00:22:20,880
Think about the way that auto insurance companies

64
00:22:20,880 --> 00:22:23,960
or not auto insurance, auto rental companies try to get you

65
00:22:23,960 --> 00:22:25,380
to buy their insurance policy.

66
00:22:26,680 --> 00:22:28,360
So, if you have your own insurance,

67
00:22:28,360 --> 00:22:29,880
you can use that, right, normally.

68
00:22:30,520 --> 00:22:33,920
But once you say that, the standard marketing technique

69
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:36,680
for a rental car agency is to say, what?

70
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:38,780
They ask you a question.

71
00:22:39,240 --> 00:22:41,620
Does your policy have a blank?

72
00:22:43,000 --> 00:22:43,620
Starts with a D.

73
00:22:43,860 --> 00:22:46,340
Does your policy have a deductible?

74
00:22:46,820 --> 00:22:48,520
And yeah, $500.

75
00:22:48,520 --> 00:22:50,360
Well, our policy doesn't have a deductible.

76
00:22:50,360 --> 00:22:52,060
So, you realize that if you get an exit,

77
00:22:52,060 --> 00:22:53,720
you'd have to pay the deductible.

78
00:22:54,380 --> 00:22:55,860
The very deductible I have to pay if I got

79
00:22:55,860 --> 00:22:57,060
into an accident in my own car?

80
00:22:57,200 --> 00:22:58,160
Yeah, I realize that.

81
00:22:58,380 --> 00:23:00,700
So what? I mean, if I didn't want a deductible,

82
00:23:00,700 --> 00:23:02,560
I wouldn't have bought a policy with a deductible

83
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:03,260
in the first place.

84
00:23:03,460 --> 00:23:05,640
I mean, then I would go and have a policy

85
00:23:05,640 --> 00:23:06,660
with no deductible all the time.

86
00:23:07,020 --> 00:23:08,720
But you want me to go and get a deductible

87
00:23:08,880 --> 00:23:11,880
for the next three days on the chance that I might have

88
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:13,740
to pay $500 while driving this car.

89
00:23:14,040 --> 00:23:15,100
Exactly. Right?

90
00:23:15,100 --> 00:23:17,780
And this, they use the sales technique all the time

91
00:23:17,780 --> 00:23:18,740
in my experience.

92
00:23:18,740 --> 00:23:20,100
I want to say half the time.

93
00:23:20,100 --> 00:23:22,260
Half the time I turn down insurance.

94
00:23:22,260 --> 00:23:24,240
They ask me whether I got a deductible and try

95
00:23:24,240 --> 00:23:25,000
to sell me that way.

96
00:23:25,000 --> 00:23:27,660
And I assume the reason why they use that sales pitch is

97
00:23:27,660 --> 00:23:29,340
because it's the most effective one they got.

98
00:23:30,580 --> 00:23:32,700
People are saying, you realize, so this way,

99
00:23:32,880 --> 00:23:34,760
if you could crash the car, destroy it,

100
00:23:34,760 --> 00:23:38,120
and you walk away, you don't know what to do.

101
00:23:38,120 --> 00:23:38,360
All right.

102
00:23:38,360 --> 00:23:40,740
But I could have bought a policy like that already,

103
00:23:40,740 --> 00:23:41,520
and I didn't want it.

104
00:23:41,900 --> 00:23:43,180
And why didn't you want it before?

105
00:23:43,180 --> 00:23:44,480
Because it's more expensive.

106
00:23:45,020 --> 00:23:45,660
And you know what?

107
00:23:45,660 --> 00:23:48,580
Those, the policy that you're paying to the auto insurance

108
00:23:48,580 --> 00:23:51,360
or to the rental car company is way more expensive per day

109
00:23:51,360 --> 00:23:52,220
than an oil policy.

110
00:23:52,880 --> 00:23:54,340
So it's especially crazy to do it.

111
00:23:54,340 --> 00:23:57,400
And on top of it, does it really, does this really get rid

112
00:23:57,400 --> 00:23:59,760
of all the risk of an auto accident, a rental car,

113
00:24:00,080 --> 00:24:02,320
if you have a policy that covers all damages?

114
00:24:03,680 --> 00:24:04,320
You could die.

115
00:24:04,720 --> 00:24:05,600
You could be maimed.

116
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:07,040
They only have everybody to sell.

117
00:24:07,200 --> 00:24:09,340
They aren't even really actually moving the risk

118
00:24:09,340 --> 00:24:10,560
down from one to zero.

119
00:24:10,900 --> 00:24:12,680
But they try to create the illusion of it

120
00:24:12,680 --> 00:24:14,800
because people love that idea so much.

121
00:24:14,800 --> 00:24:14,960
All right.

122
00:24:14,960 --> 00:24:20,880
All right.

123
00:24:20,880 --> 00:24:21,320
Yes, yes.

124
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:22,640
Although, you know, the very fact

125
00:24:22,640 --> 00:24:25,040
that they are selling it to you suggests they've got some

126
00:24:25,040 --> 00:24:26,600
actuaries and calculated the moral hazard

127
00:24:26,600 --> 00:24:29,380
and they determine it's not that, no big deal at all compared

128
00:24:29,380 --> 00:24:32,100
to the very high price per day that they are charging you.

129
00:24:33,680 --> 00:24:34,000
All right.

130
00:24:34,060 --> 00:24:34,700
So moving on.

131
00:24:35,900 --> 00:24:36,080
All right.

132
00:24:36,420 --> 00:24:39,240
One of the biggest results in behavioral economics

133
00:24:39,240 --> 00:24:42,160
and one that behavioral economists never stop talking

134
00:24:42,160 --> 00:24:45,020
about, hardly ever stop talking about,

135
00:24:45,020 --> 00:24:47,920
is that people are loss averse.

136
00:24:48,260 --> 00:24:48,860
Loss averse.

137
00:24:49,640 --> 00:24:49,880
All right.

138
00:24:49,880 --> 00:24:51,600
Now you may say, well isn't everyone loss averse?

139
00:24:52,280 --> 00:24:53,740
Not in this technical sense.

140
00:24:54,100 --> 00:24:54,960
Not in this technical sense.

141
00:24:55,780 --> 00:24:59,800
So in particular, in basic consumer theory,

142
00:25:00,480 --> 00:25:02,420
preferences and endowments are totally separate.

143
00:25:02,860 --> 00:25:05,720
So you have a utility function which doesn't have any reference

144
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:07,720
to what, to your initial possessions.

145
00:25:08,840 --> 00:25:09,140
All right.

146
00:25:09,140 --> 00:25:12,860
But the idea of loss aversion, again in this technical sense,

147
00:25:13,340 --> 00:25:16,460
is that losing something that you started with is worse

148
00:25:16,460 --> 00:25:17,980
than never having had it at all.

149
00:25:19,040 --> 00:25:20,380
That's the way loss aversion works.

150
00:25:20,760 --> 00:25:23,180
So if you get a box of candy for your birthday and then you drop

151
00:25:23,180 --> 00:25:24,900
it in the mud, you'll feel worse

152
00:25:24,900 --> 00:25:25,820
than if you didn't get a present.

153
00:25:26,980 --> 00:25:27,620
That's the idea.

154
00:25:28,200 --> 00:25:29,600
You know, if you have an, if you,

155
00:25:29,600 --> 00:25:31,760
if your car gets a nice new paint job

156
00:25:31,760 --> 00:25:33,900
and then it gets scratched, you feel worse

157
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,320
than if you just didn't get the paint job

158
00:25:36,320 --> 00:25:39,500
and the car was scratched all along.

159
00:25:40,820 --> 00:25:40,980
All right.

160
00:25:40,980 --> 00:25:41,940
So that's the idea.

161
00:25:41,940 --> 00:25:44,500
And again, so loss doesn't mean that you don't,

162
00:25:44,500 --> 00:25:46,500
it means that you don't like losing things

163
00:25:46,500 --> 00:25:47,280
that you currently have.

164
00:25:48,020 --> 00:25:52,440
In other words, your utility depends in part upon the match

165
00:25:52,440 --> 00:25:54,160
between what you currently have

166
00:25:54,360 --> 00:25:56,440
and what you think you ought to have or what you started with.

167
00:25:58,380 --> 00:26:00,180
Okay. Which is again a pretty intuitive idea.

168
00:26:00,180 --> 00:26:02,640
And if you know small children, this makes perfect sense.

169
00:26:03,180 --> 00:26:05,240
Because, you know, as I think I mentioned,

170
00:26:05,240 --> 00:26:09,360
but worth repeating, if you want a child to throw a tantrum,

171
00:26:09,660 --> 00:26:12,120
a great thing to do is to give them something nice

172
00:26:12,340 --> 00:26:14,860
and then let them play with it for an hour and then say, okay,

173
00:26:14,860 --> 00:26:15,640
it's mine again.

174
00:26:15,640 --> 00:26:16,420
Now it's mine again.

175
00:26:16,420 --> 00:26:18,100
You lose it, you know, I'm taking it away from you.

176
00:26:18,100 --> 00:26:21,220
And that kid will be way more miserable

177
00:26:21,440 --> 00:26:23,340
than if the child had never given the thing.

178
00:26:24,560 --> 00:26:26,520
If you want to reduce the risk of this,

179
00:26:26,520 --> 00:26:28,600
there's no guarantees here, but if you want to reduce the risk,

180
00:26:28,980 --> 00:26:31,800
you very clearly tell the child, this is my toy.

181
00:26:32,040 --> 00:26:34,340
It belongs to me and to me alone.

182
00:26:34,340 --> 00:26:39,460
But I'm going to be nice and allow you, a non-owner

183
00:26:39,460 --> 00:26:43,440
of this good, to play with it for a small amount of time.

184
00:26:44,260 --> 00:26:45,580
Then I'm going to ask for it back.

185
00:26:45,900 --> 00:26:47,100
You must give it back to me.

186
00:26:47,380 --> 00:26:49,360
If you complain, I will never give you anything

187
00:26:49,360 --> 00:26:49,980
like this again.

188
00:26:50,700 --> 00:26:53,900
Right? And then you reduce your risk of a tantrum from like 99%

189
00:26:54,340 --> 00:26:55,920
down to maybe 40%.

190
00:26:56,700 --> 00:26:56,960
All right?

191
00:26:57,400 --> 00:27:01,480
So it's a big game, but still, like the whole sales pitch is

192
00:27:01,480 --> 00:27:04,940
to try to convince the person that though they are playing

193
00:27:04,940 --> 00:27:06,940
with the toy, it isn't their toy.

194
00:27:07,540 --> 00:27:07,740
Yes?

195
00:27:07,980 --> 00:27:09,440
This is one of the techniques of the stoics.

196
00:27:09,840 --> 00:27:14,380
Good, yes, great analogy, the stoics and Epicure.

197
00:27:14,580 --> 00:27:15,900
Things that are not in your control,

198
00:27:16,180 --> 00:27:17,540
you identify as not your own.

199
00:27:18,020 --> 00:27:18,580
Right, right, yes.

200
00:27:18,580 --> 00:27:21,480
So if you have a cup that you love, you say, this is my cup.

201
00:27:21,820 --> 00:27:22,940
It's going to break at some point.

202
00:27:23,940 --> 00:27:24,640
Yeah, that's very good.

203
00:27:24,640 --> 00:27:25,440
It's just here for a time.

204
00:27:25,440 --> 00:27:28,440
And it's also very much in the philosophy of Epicurus.

205
00:27:29,060 --> 00:27:30,440
So today, Epicure means someone

206
00:27:30,440 --> 00:27:32,440
who really appreciates fine wine and stuff like that.

207
00:27:32,440 --> 00:27:34,200
But Epicurus was nothing like that at all.

208
00:27:34,580 --> 00:27:36,320
Epicurus was someone who advised everyone

209
00:27:36,320 --> 00:27:38,480
to lower their expectations to the minimum level

210
00:27:38,780 --> 00:27:40,300
so they'd be happy all the time.

211
00:27:41,120 --> 00:27:43,840
Like every day, basically you expect, well,

212
00:27:43,840 --> 00:27:45,820
every day you say, well, look, my expectation is

213
00:27:45,820 --> 00:27:47,160
to not be horribly tortured today.

214
00:27:47,780 --> 00:27:48,960
And every day it doesn't happen.

215
00:27:48,960 --> 00:27:50,520
You're like, hey, this is fantastic.

216
00:27:51,720 --> 00:27:54,680
It's all like I got way more than not being horribly tortured.

217
00:27:54,680 --> 00:27:55,720
I had meals.

218
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:57,420
I had a conversation with friends.

219
00:27:57,420 --> 00:27:58,720
I went for a pleasant stroll.

220
00:27:58,720 --> 00:28:00,940
And so we get the same idea

221
00:28:00,940 --> 00:28:03,880
where if you just make your expectations really low,

222
00:28:04,120 --> 00:28:04,980
then they're easily met.

223
00:28:04,980 --> 00:28:08,340
And then so much of happiness is not about absolute levels.

224
00:28:08,340 --> 00:28:10,720
It's about disparities between what you think you're entitled

225
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:11,820
to and what you've actually got.

226
00:28:14,180 --> 00:28:15,620
Okay. All right.

227
00:28:15,620 --> 00:28:21,780
Anyway, this insight that people are loss aversion is the root

228
00:28:21,820 --> 00:28:24,300
of the main theoretical innovation

229
00:28:24,300 --> 00:28:25,260
of behavioral economics.

230
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:26,220
All right.

231
00:28:26,280 --> 00:28:27,060
What do I mean by this?

232
00:28:27,060 --> 00:28:28,440
I mean a new theory.

233
00:28:28,440 --> 00:28:31,620
Normally, behavioral economists are just people poking holes

234
00:28:31,620 --> 00:28:33,360
in the standard theory and saying, you know,

235
00:28:33,360 --> 00:28:34,560
that theory doesn't work.

236
00:28:34,560 --> 00:28:36,760
Ha ha. And there's another theory that doesn't work.

237
00:28:36,760 --> 00:28:37,300
And another one.

238
00:28:37,300 --> 00:28:38,880
It's got five different holes.

239
00:28:39,440 --> 00:28:39,740
All right.

240
00:28:39,740 --> 00:28:42,100
Now, it's easy to sit around poking holes in theories, right?

241
00:28:42,620 --> 00:28:44,740
But much harder to come up with a new theory

242
00:28:44,740 --> 00:28:45,960
that accurately describes the world.

243
00:28:47,140 --> 00:28:49,920
Normally, behavioral economists just realize how hard that's

244
00:28:49,920 --> 00:28:50,860
going to be if they don't do it.

245
00:28:51,580 --> 00:28:55,280
But there is a theory that behavioral economists are well

246
00:28:55,280 --> 00:28:57,140
associated with, which is called prospect theory,

247
00:28:57,140 --> 00:29:02,540
where they actually do try to explain the data that they got.

248
00:29:02,540 --> 00:29:05,500
And we're going to see how successful they are.

249
00:29:06,720 --> 00:29:07,460
All right.

250
00:29:07,680 --> 00:29:08,780
So prospect theory.

251
00:29:13,820 --> 00:29:14,080
All right.

252
00:29:15,140 --> 00:29:18,220
So the idea of prospect theory is that we get rid

253
00:29:18,220 --> 00:29:22,600
of this regular old expected utility curve.

254
00:29:22,600 --> 00:29:26,920
And notice, does that look kind of like that one?

255
00:29:28,260 --> 00:29:28,900
Yes. All right.

256
00:29:28,900 --> 00:29:31,320
So this is your usual risk-averse curve,

257
00:29:31,740 --> 00:29:35,140
where if you go and draw a line between any two points,

258
00:29:35,640 --> 00:29:37,200
the curve is above the lines,

259
00:29:37,500 --> 00:29:40,160
indicating that people prefer a sure thing to a gamma.

260
00:29:41,380 --> 00:29:44,780
Right? Indicating that if you could have the expected value

261
00:29:45,360 --> 00:29:50,020
of this chord here, you would rather have the expected value

262
00:29:50,020 --> 00:29:53,400
rather than take a probability of a certain chance of that,

263
00:29:53,400 --> 00:29:55,140
a certain chance of that, which is the same average value.

264
00:29:56,800 --> 00:29:57,840
Okay? All right.

265
00:29:57,840 --> 00:29:58,980
Now, that's the normal thing.

266
00:29:59,440 --> 00:30:02,800
What prospect theory does is get rid of that curve and replace it

267
00:30:02,800 --> 00:30:04,360
with an NS-shaped curve.

268
00:30:05,700 --> 00:30:08,160
So that's classic prospect theory.

269
00:30:10,900 --> 00:30:12,680
What's so special about this curve?

270
00:30:13,340 --> 00:30:13,660
All right.

271
00:30:13,700 --> 00:30:18,780
Well, the idea here is that that is like your status quo point.

272
00:30:18,780 --> 00:30:21,660
That is, so that point there is the status quo point.

273
00:30:21,980 --> 00:30:24,080
So that's where you start off.

274
00:30:24,400 --> 00:30:26,700
That's sort of like the amount of wealth that you take

275
00:30:26,700 --> 00:30:28,300
for granted that you feel entitled to.

276
00:30:28,900 --> 00:30:34,320
And then what it says is that for amounts of wealth above what

277
00:30:34,320 --> 00:30:36,380
you take for granted, then you have normal risk aversion.

278
00:30:37,000 --> 00:30:39,140
Right? You see how it is that same concave shape?

279
00:30:40,380 --> 00:30:43,700
So it says that if you have a chance to get extra stuff,

280
00:30:44,300 --> 00:30:45,800
if you have a chance of getting more stuff,

281
00:30:45,800 --> 00:30:48,060
then you're risk-averse, and then you'd rather be certain

282
00:30:48,060 --> 00:30:50,860
of getting the extra stuff, of getting an average

283
00:30:50,860 --> 00:30:54,100
of the possibilities to take your chances.

284
00:30:54,880 --> 00:30:59,160
But below the status quo point, below this point

285
00:30:59,240 --> 00:31:01,660
where you define your expectations,

286
00:31:02,200 --> 00:31:03,180
that is the other way around.

287
00:31:03,920 --> 00:31:06,940
Here, at this point, you'd rather actually take the gamble.

288
00:31:08,260 --> 00:31:09,640
You'd rather actually take the gamble.

289
00:31:09,640 --> 00:31:14,640
So this says that if there is, that if you're, if there's a,

290
00:31:14,640 --> 00:31:17,780
if you're going to lose money, you would rather go

291
00:31:17,780 --> 00:31:20,440
and take a gamble where you have a chance of losing a ton of money

292
00:31:20,440 --> 00:31:22,280
but another possibility of not losing anything,

293
00:31:22,720 --> 00:31:26,520
versus just taking a, an average loss for sure.

294
00:31:28,640 --> 00:31:33,940
So in prospect theory, you are risk-averse above the loss

295
00:31:33,940 --> 00:31:37,760
point, but you are risk-preferring below the

296
00:31:37,760 --> 00:31:38,280
loss point.

297
00:31:40,000 --> 00:31:40,340
All right.

298
00:31:41,200 --> 00:31:44,560
So what's the point of this?

299
00:31:44,560 --> 00:31:47,920
Well, this is to say things like, well, sure,

300
00:31:48,200 --> 00:31:52,340
when someone is going, has a chance of getting a game,

301
00:31:52,640 --> 00:31:54,260
then they're going to want insurance,

302
00:31:54,260 --> 00:31:55,220
then they want certainty.

303
00:31:55,460 --> 00:32:00,020
On the other hand, for a loss, people will take big chances

304
00:32:00,020 --> 00:32:01,600
because they don't want to lose anything.

305
00:32:02,280 --> 00:32:04,020
Right? And it's really important not to lose anything,

306
00:32:04,020 --> 00:32:07,400
so they would take, you know, so you'd either lose $1,000

307
00:32:07,400 --> 00:32:10,440
for sure, or you could have a 50% chance of losing $3,000

308
00:32:10,440 --> 00:32:11,980
and a 50% chance of losing nothing.

309
00:32:11,980 --> 00:32:16,000
And someone with, who satisfies this prospect theory,

310
00:32:16,260 --> 00:32:17,520
would like that second gamble.

311
00:32:17,860 --> 00:32:18,520
They'd be interested.

312
00:32:19,340 --> 00:32:20,860
He's like, so then there's a 50% chance,

313
00:32:20,860 --> 00:32:23,460
what 50% chance I won't lose anything at all?

314
00:32:23,700 --> 00:32:25,340
Awesome. Okay, I want to take that.

315
00:32:25,340 --> 00:32:26,880
And then, of course, they really don't like it

316
00:32:26,880 --> 00:32:29,580
when they lose $3,000, but still, they want the gamble

317
00:32:29,580 --> 00:32:31,580
where they have a 50% chance of not losing anything.

318
00:32:33,080 --> 00:32:33,340
All right.

319
00:32:34,120 --> 00:32:38,200
Now, this is the simple version of prospect theory.

320
00:32:38,680 --> 00:32:41,060
Now, if you're thinking about it for a little while,

321
00:32:41,060 --> 00:32:43,820
and again, this is the kind of thing where people came

322
00:32:43,820 --> 00:32:46,940
up with this really smart, and it takes so little time to come

323
00:32:46,940 --> 00:32:48,740
up with seemingly devastating objections

324
00:32:49,380 --> 00:32:51,280
that you wonder what's going on,

325
00:32:51,280 --> 00:32:52,640
and I don't have any good story for that.

326
00:32:53,080 --> 00:32:56,080
But let's just take a look at this curve.

327
00:32:56,160 --> 00:32:58,300
Prospect theory, supposedly the great theory

328
00:32:58,300 --> 00:33:00,420
that shows how wrong expected utility theory is.

329
00:33:00,920 --> 00:33:04,120
And prospect theory says that people risk a person gains,

330
00:33:04,460 --> 00:33:05,840
but risk preferring over losses.

331
00:33:06,380 --> 00:33:08,520
And therefore, what do you want to,

332
00:33:08,520 --> 00:33:10,180
well, when do you want to buy insurance?

333
00:33:10,180 --> 00:33:11,360
Do you want to buy insurance for gains

334
00:33:11,360 --> 00:33:12,860
or losses according to prospect theory?

335
00:33:13,740 --> 00:33:16,300
You want to buy insurance for gains.

336
00:33:17,100 --> 00:33:17,540
All right.

337
00:33:17,900 --> 00:33:20,260
Now, let's think about all known forms of insurance.

338
00:33:21,780 --> 00:33:23,760
Fire insurance, is that for gains or losses?

339
00:33:23,960 --> 00:33:26,360
Auto insurance, gains or losses?

340
00:33:26,360 --> 00:33:28,380
Health insurance, gains or losses?

341
00:33:28,980 --> 00:33:30,600
Life insurance, gains or losses?

342
00:33:31,260 --> 00:33:33,660
Every kind of insurance that I've ever heard of and bought

343
00:33:33,660 --> 00:33:37,040
like virtually is actually for the kind

344
00:33:37,040 --> 00:33:41,420
that prospect theory says people don't want insurance for.

345
00:33:41,660 --> 00:33:42,080
All right.

346
00:33:42,580 --> 00:33:45,700
And then on top of it, there's also another puzzle.

347
00:33:45,840 --> 00:33:48,140
How about gambling, casinos, lotteries?

348
00:33:48,500 --> 00:33:49,100
What are those?

349
00:33:50,520 --> 00:33:53,380
Gains. And what are people doing when they go to Las Vegas

350
00:33:53,380 --> 00:33:55,100
or when they buy a lottery ticket?

351
00:33:56,120 --> 00:33:58,940
Is they are acting risk preferring because, of course,

352
00:33:58,940 --> 00:34:01,700
the reason those casinos exist is because they don't pay

353
00:34:02,180 --> 00:34:03,680
as much on average as you put in.

354
00:34:03,680 --> 00:34:07,120
So far from being a more beautiful theory,

355
00:34:07,120 --> 00:34:09,920
it just creates two giant new categories of error.

356
00:34:11,040 --> 00:34:15,860
So it's weird that anyone ever found this remotely plausible

357
00:34:15,860 --> 00:34:16,480
to me anyway.

358
00:34:16,480 --> 00:34:18,900
I don't know why it ever had any appeal.

359
00:34:18,900 --> 00:34:20,000
All right.

360
00:34:20,000 --> 00:34:21,660
Now, I'm a crock.

361
00:34:21,660 --> 00:34:23,740
Now, of course, I'm not the only person to notice this.

362
00:34:24,280 --> 00:34:28,100
And as a result of this total and humiliating failure

363
00:34:28,100 --> 00:34:31,380
of simple prospect theory, there is advanced prospect theory.

364
00:34:31,380 --> 00:34:32,800
All right.

365
00:34:32,800 --> 00:34:35,020
Advanced prospect theory does get rid

366
00:34:35,020 --> 00:34:37,000
of these problems in the following way.

367
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:41,220
All right.

368
00:34:41,220 --> 00:34:43,120
It says, all right, look, we are wrong to try

369
00:34:43,120 --> 00:34:44,600
to make this a two-dimensional model,

370
00:34:44,600 --> 00:34:46,600
to make this a simple model here

371
00:34:46,600 --> 00:34:49,700
where there's just a set of square point above and below.

372
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:52,580
Really, what we need is what is called the four-fold theory.

373
00:34:53,340 --> 00:34:54,240
The four-fold theory.

374
00:34:54,800 --> 00:34:55,020
All right.

375
00:34:55,020 --> 00:34:57,860
The four-fold theory says that people are all the following.

376
00:34:57,860 --> 00:35:01,660
First of all, risk aversion gains with high probabilities.

377
00:35:02,580 --> 00:35:05,800
So this says, for example, that people, that, you know,

378
00:35:05,800 --> 00:35:09,780
that if you are coming into an inheritance, say,

379
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:12,020
people would go and buy insurance to make sure

380
00:35:12,020 --> 00:35:14,200
that policy where they have a 90% chance

381
00:35:14,200 --> 00:35:15,540
of becomes 100% chance.

382
00:35:16,440 --> 00:35:19,820
So basically, people will pay to, you know, will pay to get rid

383
00:35:19,820 --> 00:35:22,020
of a small risk so that they know they're getting a gain

384
00:35:22,020 --> 00:35:22,340
for sure.

385
00:35:22,340 --> 00:35:23,500
All right.

386
00:35:23,500 --> 00:35:24,100
So let's go do that.

387
00:35:24,600 --> 00:35:26,680
It says people are risking gains with low probabilities.

388
00:35:26,680 --> 00:35:28,800
So this says that people will buy lottery tickets

389
00:35:28,800 --> 00:35:29,640
and go to Las Vegas.

390
00:35:30,380 --> 00:35:30,640
All right.

391
00:35:30,640 --> 00:35:32,780
Then, they're risk averse and loss of low probabilities,

392
00:35:32,780 --> 00:35:34,240
so that's why they buy normal insurance.

393
00:35:34,240 --> 00:35:37,140
And then finally, they're risk seeking and loss

394
00:35:37,140 --> 00:35:40,640
of high probabilities, so that's why, say,

395
00:35:40,640 --> 00:35:47,220
people might pretend that their car was stolen rather

396
00:35:47,220 --> 00:35:48,160
than admit that they crashed it

397
00:35:48,160 --> 00:35:49,260
so the ride doesn't get mad at them.

398
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:52,100
Right? So like, well, it's pretty likely that she's going

399
00:35:52,100 --> 00:35:55,340
to go and get mad at me if I admit that I crashed the car.

400
00:35:55,340 --> 00:35:57,940
So I'll make up a lie, which actually is probably going

401
00:35:57,940 --> 00:36:01,160
to fail too, but I'm just going to go and take this chance

402
00:36:01,160 --> 00:36:04,260
of completely escaping all dissatisfaction.

403
00:36:05,060 --> 00:36:05,320
All right.

404
00:36:06,140 --> 00:36:07,920
So that is the fourfold theory.

405
00:36:07,920 --> 00:36:09,080
It's gotten a lot of attention.

406
00:36:09,500 --> 00:36:11,940
Now, remember, this is supposed

407
00:36:11,940 --> 00:36:13,460
to be an empirically testable theory.

408
00:36:13,460 --> 00:36:15,640
Now, when you look at this fourfold,

409
00:36:15,640 --> 00:36:17,080
let's look at the simple one.

410
00:36:17,380 --> 00:36:18,720
That one is not so hard to figure

411
00:36:18,720 --> 00:36:20,080
out how you test empirically, right?

412
00:36:21,460 --> 00:36:23,880
Because it says, look, people are risk preferring

413
00:36:23,880 --> 00:36:28,120
if they're in games and they are risk aversing games,

414
00:36:28,120 --> 00:36:30,040
risk preferring losses, then all you need

415
00:36:30,040 --> 00:36:32,460
to know is what their status quo is.

416
00:36:34,100 --> 00:36:35,040
So where do they start?

417
00:36:35,500 --> 00:36:37,900
And then you know, and then we look at what happens

418
00:36:37,900 --> 00:36:39,340
when a change happens, and it's pretty simple.

419
00:36:40,180 --> 00:36:44,440
But for this fourfold theory, we need to know what counts

420
00:36:44,440 --> 00:36:48,960
as not only gain or loss, but we also need to go

421
00:36:48,960 --> 00:36:54,380
and define the cutoffs for high versus low probability.

422
00:36:54,380 --> 00:36:56,980
And notice, from the description,

423
00:36:56,980 --> 00:37:00,160
I would say in a practice, outside of a lab,

424
00:37:00,160 --> 00:37:02,740
it's pretty easy for someone to just call something high

425
00:37:02,740 --> 00:37:05,040
or low depending upon how they need it to work out.

426
00:37:05,840 --> 00:37:08,180
Right? So like is a 20% chance of a loss a high

427
00:37:08,180 --> 00:37:09,340
or low probability of a loss?

428
00:37:11,000 --> 00:37:11,580
I don't know.

429
00:37:11,860 --> 00:37:14,020
I haven't seen anyone who likes prospect theory go

430
00:37:14,020 --> 00:37:17,800
and say what the cut point is for which theory applies.

431
00:37:17,800 --> 00:37:20,440
So this actually is much closer

432
00:37:20,440 --> 00:37:21,800
to a theory that explains everything.

433
00:37:23,180 --> 00:37:25,140
In a lab, of course, you could set it up to make sure

434
00:37:25,140 --> 00:37:28,480
so that there's one probability is higher than the other one.

435
00:37:28,760 --> 00:37:33,060
So if someone is risk averse in one probability,

436
00:37:33,060 --> 00:37:34,300
then they better be risk averse

437
00:37:34,300 --> 00:37:36,320
in an even higher probability, for example.

438
00:37:36,320 --> 00:37:37,560
So you can do that in a lab.

439
00:37:37,560 --> 00:37:39,280
But in the real world, what does this really say?

440
00:37:39,280 --> 00:37:41,620
I mean, to me, it's just sometimes people risk averse,

441
00:37:41,620 --> 00:37:42,600
sometimes they risk preferring.

442
00:37:43,460 --> 00:37:45,480
And I don't see that it adds that much more.

443
00:37:45,480 --> 00:37:45,660
All right.

444
00:37:45,660 --> 00:37:48,640
All right.

445
00:37:48,980 --> 00:37:49,720
Questions?

446
00:37:49,800 --> 00:37:50,580
Questions?

447
00:37:51,100 --> 00:37:51,540
All right.

448
00:37:52,440 --> 00:37:54,500
Then last topic, intertemporal anomalies.

449
00:37:55,120 --> 00:37:55,420
All right.

450
00:37:55,420 --> 00:37:57,020
So remember current capital markets?

451
00:37:57,020 --> 00:37:57,900
All right.

452
00:37:57,900 --> 00:37:58,580
I'm going to ask Scott Burdick.

453
00:37:58,580 --> 00:37:59,660
I don't know if you remember current capital markets.

454
00:37:59,660 --> 00:37:59,760
Sure.

455
00:37:59,760 --> 00:37:59,820
Yes.

456
00:37:59,820 --> 00:38:16,880
I have a question actually.

457
00:38:16,880 --> 00:38:19,460
Like we were saying that if you were

458
00:38:19,460 --> 00:38:21,220
to test the simple prospect theory,

459
00:38:21,220 --> 00:38:22,780
isn't that like the status quo point

460
00:38:22,780 --> 00:38:23,940
for each different individual?

461
00:38:23,940 --> 00:38:25,380
Like they're also like very subjective.

462
00:38:25,380 --> 00:38:25,980
You can't really.

463
00:38:25,980 --> 00:38:26,820
That is another good point.

464
00:38:26,820 --> 00:38:27,580
So again, of course, in a lab,

465
00:38:27,580 --> 00:38:28,880
you can just say the status quo is whatever amount

466
00:38:28,880 --> 00:38:29,380
of money we start.

467
00:38:29,380 --> 00:38:31,860
But I would say that with, you know, certainly with kids,

468
00:38:31,860 --> 00:38:34,220
like with younger kids, I have a very good sense

469
00:38:34,220 --> 00:38:37,340
of what they consider their starting point.

470
00:38:37,780 --> 00:38:40,100
So, you know, like if they've never touched a toy before,

471
00:38:40,100 --> 00:38:41,040
they don't think that's their toy.

472
00:38:41,720 --> 00:38:43,920
If they bring a toy with them to your house,

473
00:38:43,920 --> 00:38:45,880
they think that's their toy.

474
00:38:46,240 --> 00:38:50,020
For adults, I mean, so think about your ability

475
00:38:50,020 --> 00:38:53,520
to predict whether an adult will feel, will say, hey, that's mine.

476
00:38:54,680 --> 00:38:57,140
Or, you know, so I feel like I have a pretty good,

477
00:38:57,140 --> 00:39:00,560
I can make pretty good predictions about that.

478
00:39:00,560 --> 00:39:03,180
And often it will not actually fit legal rules.

479
00:39:03,720 --> 00:39:06,060
So, you know, things like, hey, that's my parking spot.

480
00:39:06,800 --> 00:39:07,800
I don't see a name on it.

481
00:39:08,260 --> 00:39:10,520
And yet people will consider something their parking spot.

482
00:39:10,520 --> 00:39:12,840
And in fact, I think you have a pretty good idea

483
00:39:12,840 --> 00:39:14,560
for when someone will consider a parking spot their

484
00:39:14,560 --> 00:39:15,200
parking spot.

485
00:39:16,700 --> 00:39:18,560
So, I mean, like there is a subjective element.

486
00:39:18,560 --> 00:39:19,500
I don't think it's that hard.

487
00:39:20,180 --> 00:39:21,680
But, like that could just be me.

488
00:39:21,680 --> 00:39:22,520
I could be wrong on that.

489
00:39:22,520 --> 00:39:24,300
So maybe there is more subjectivity.

490
00:39:24,300 --> 00:39:27,320
If it were that subjective, people would be getting

491
00:39:27,320 --> 00:39:30,340
into shouting matches at worst very frequently.

492
00:39:30,680 --> 00:39:33,180
Because if there was some disagreement

493
00:39:33,180 --> 00:39:36,540
about whether the stuff in your wallet is yours, that would be,

494
00:39:36,680 --> 00:39:38,040
like whether that phone is yours.

495
00:39:38,040 --> 00:39:40,540
If I were to say, hey, I don't see your name on this phone.

496
00:39:40,540 --> 00:39:42,540
It's like, it's in the phone.

497
00:39:42,720 --> 00:39:43,360
In the phone?

498
00:39:43,360 --> 00:39:43,980
What does that mean?

499
00:39:43,980 --> 00:39:45,320
It has to be, no, no, no, it has to be engraved

500
00:39:45,320 --> 00:39:46,380
on the phone or it doesn't count.

501
00:39:47,100 --> 00:39:49,060
Right? If I'm like, yeah, I could have these theories.

502
00:39:49,060 --> 00:39:51,520
But I think all, everyone here would disagree with the theory

503
00:39:51,520 --> 00:39:53,760
that your phone must have your name engraved on it

504
00:39:53,760 --> 00:39:55,280
in order for it to be yours.

505
00:39:55,280 --> 00:39:57,240
And otherwise, it's finders keepers.

506
00:39:57,240 --> 00:39:57,420
Right?

507
00:39:57,920 --> 00:39:58,000
Yeah.

508
00:39:58,000 --> 00:39:59,800
You can't actually do that.

509
00:39:59,800 --> 00:40:24,800
You can't.


1
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:02,000
I actually agree with that.

2
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:04,000
Yeah, so some people agree with it,

3
00:40:04,000 --> 00:40:06,000
but the rule is not if you don't agree with it,

4
00:40:06,000 --> 00:40:09,000
then anyone who agrees to have it and claim it as their own phone.

5
00:40:09,000 --> 00:40:11,000
All right. So, moving on.

6
00:40:11,000 --> 00:40:12,000
So, intertemporal anomalies.

7
00:40:12,000 --> 00:40:14,000
So, supposing you have perfect capital markets,

8
00:40:14,000 --> 00:40:16,000
we don't in the real world, but if you do,

9
00:40:16,000 --> 00:40:19,000
then basic microeconomics is a very simple theory

10
00:40:19,000 --> 00:40:21,000
about how people discount the future,

11
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:24,000
which is everyone will just use the rate of interest.

12
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:27,000
Because if you have perfect capital markets,

13
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:30,000
and if you were discounting an asset at more than the interest rate,

14
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:32,000
what should you do?

15
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:38,000
So, you know, like suppose you have an asset,

16
00:40:38,000 --> 00:40:40,000
which is good for $1,000 in a year,

17
00:40:40,000 --> 00:40:43,000
and you discounted it 7%, but the market interest rate is 5%.

18
00:40:43,000 --> 00:40:45,000
What should you immediately do?

19
00:40:45,000 --> 00:40:46,000
Sell the asset.

20
00:40:46,000 --> 00:40:48,000
Sell the asset. And how much money will you get for it?

21
00:40:48,000 --> 00:40:51,000
Like $950, but you valued it like $930.

22
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:53,000
So, you made an instant profit.

23
00:40:53,000 --> 00:40:56,000
All right. So, that's how perfect capital markets work.

24
00:40:56,000 --> 00:41:00,000
So, anyone that has a value different from the market interest rate

25
00:41:00,000 --> 00:41:03,000
should either go and sell it if they discounted it

26
00:41:03,000 --> 00:41:05,000
at a higher amount than the market interest rate,

27
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:08,000
or they should go and buy more if they discounted it

28
00:41:08,000 --> 00:41:10,000
at less than the market interest rate.

29
00:41:10,000 --> 00:41:12,000
Very simple. Right?

30
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:14,000
And even if you got imperfect capital markets,

31
00:41:14,000 --> 00:41:17,000
there are often some definite predictions

32
00:41:17,000 --> 00:41:20,000
of intertemporal choice about what people do, as well as you.

33
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:23,000
Now, microeconomists have found a number of anomalies.

34
00:41:23,000 --> 00:41:27,000
All right. So, first one.

35
00:41:27,000 --> 00:41:32,000
This is possibly the clearest cut case in all,

36
00:41:32,000 --> 00:41:34,000
certainly in all the intertemporal anomalies,

37
00:41:34,000 --> 00:41:36,000
maybe even in all behavioral economics.

38
00:41:36,000 --> 00:41:39,000
All right. There are a lot of people who have given a choice

39
00:41:39,000 --> 00:41:42,000
between being paid over 12 months or over 9 months,

40
00:41:42,000 --> 00:41:44,000
starting now, will actually say,

41
00:41:44,000 --> 00:41:47,000
I want to be paid over 12 months.

42
00:41:47,000 --> 00:41:50,000
If there's any positive interest rate, this makes no sense. Right?

43
00:41:50,000 --> 00:41:53,000
Because what has a higher present value?

44
00:41:53,000 --> 00:41:56,000
Yes. You got money today, so getting paid over 9 months

45
00:41:56,000 --> 00:41:59,000
is a higher present value than getting over paid over 12 months.

46
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:03,000
George Mason, when I arrived here, gave me this option,

47
00:42:03,000 --> 00:42:06,000
and I did what economic theory says people do.

48
00:42:06,000 --> 00:42:08,000
So I asked to be paid over 9 months,

49
00:42:08,000 --> 00:42:09,000
so I'm still being paid over 9 months.

50
00:42:09,000 --> 00:42:11,000
About 15 years ago, they got rid of the option,

51
00:42:11,000 --> 00:42:14,000
grandfathering in people like me.

52
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:16,000
And when they were asked, why did you get rid of it,

53
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:18,000
what do you think they said?

54
00:42:18,000 --> 00:42:20,000
Nobody chose it. No one chose it.

55
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:22,000
No one chooses it except for... Economists.

56
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:26,000
Yes, people making lots of money.

57
00:42:26,000 --> 00:42:28,000
I'm a little nervous that if I looked into it,

58
00:42:28,000 --> 00:42:31,000
a third of the economists might have made the wrong choice.

59
00:42:31,000 --> 00:42:34,000
So the math people don't do it either?

60
00:42:34,000 --> 00:42:38,000
So I'm just repeating the story

61
00:42:38,000 --> 00:42:41,000
that I've been repeating for years.

62
00:42:41,000 --> 00:42:44,000
So I would be curious about whether the math people do it, actually.

63
00:42:44,000 --> 00:42:47,000
Based upon math editions that I know, who aren't economists,

64
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:49,000
I think they would do the normal thing.

65
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:51,000
They'd say, let's math.

66
00:42:51,000 --> 00:42:55,000
This is... life.

67
00:42:55,000 --> 00:42:57,000
Alright.

68
00:42:57,000 --> 00:42:59,000
So anyway, that seemed...

69
00:42:59,000 --> 00:43:01,000
And of course, what are the normal human thing to say

70
00:43:01,000 --> 00:43:03,000
about why you want to get paid over 12 months

71
00:43:03,000 --> 00:43:05,000
rather than 9 months?

72
00:43:05,000 --> 00:43:08,000
I might run out of money.

73
00:43:08,000 --> 00:43:11,000
Well, there's a really easy fix for that.

74
00:43:11,000 --> 00:43:16,000
Don't spend more than 1 twelfth of your annual money per month.

75
00:43:16,000 --> 00:43:18,000
It's really hard.

76
00:43:18,000 --> 00:43:21,000
But I might go and be tempted to do the wrong thing.

77
00:43:21,000 --> 00:43:24,000
Don't give in to temptation. Do the right thing.

78
00:43:24,000 --> 00:43:25,000
Very simple.

79
00:43:25,000 --> 00:43:29,000
But a lot of people seem to be so discussful of themselves

80
00:43:29,000 --> 00:43:31,000
they don't want to do that.

81
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:32,000
Alright.

82
00:43:32,000 --> 00:43:34,000
Now another very similar thing, actually,

83
00:43:34,000 --> 00:43:36,000
and on a much larger scale.

84
00:43:36,000 --> 00:43:41,000
There are a lot of people who deliberately receive tax refunds.

85
00:43:41,000 --> 00:43:44,000
Which reminds me, actually, to avoid a fine on my taxes,

86
00:43:44,000 --> 00:43:46,000
I need to increase my withholding.

87
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:48,000
So I'm doing the sensible thing,

88
00:43:48,000 --> 00:43:50,000
but if you do it 2 years in a row,

89
00:43:50,000 --> 00:43:53,000
then it's no longer sensible.

90
00:43:53,000 --> 00:43:55,000
If this makes no sense to you,

91
00:43:55,000 --> 00:43:59,000
then you probably are having too much money withheld from your taxes.

92
00:43:59,000 --> 00:44:02,000
But anyway, so the way that it works

93
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:06,000
is that if you go and have more money withheld from your taxes

94
00:44:06,000 --> 00:44:08,000
than you owe to the government at the end of the year,

95
00:44:08,000 --> 00:44:10,000
then they go and give you a tax refund.

96
00:44:10,000 --> 00:44:12,000
And isn't it wonderful to get a tax refund check?

97
00:44:12,000 --> 00:44:14,000
It's so much fun. I got my tax refund.

98
00:44:14,000 --> 00:44:15,000
You can sing a song.

99
00:44:15,000 --> 00:44:18,000
But of course, there's something you could have done

100
00:44:18,000 --> 00:44:19,000
to have not received that refund.

101
00:44:19,000 --> 00:44:22,000
What can you do to not receive the refund?

102
00:44:22,000 --> 00:44:24,000
Reduce your withholding.

103
00:44:24,000 --> 00:44:26,000
You reduce your withholding.

104
00:44:26,000 --> 00:44:28,000
And when you reduce your withholding,

105
00:44:28,000 --> 00:44:30,000
when do you get the refund?

106
00:44:30,000 --> 00:44:32,000
Earlier.

107
00:44:32,000 --> 00:44:34,000
And how much interest does the government pay you

108
00:44:34,000 --> 00:44:36,000
when it gives you your tax refund?

109
00:44:36,000 --> 00:44:38,000
0% nominal.

110
00:44:38,000 --> 00:44:41,000
0% nominal interest rate.

111
00:44:41,000 --> 00:44:44,000
Furthermore, actually, what you really want to do

112
00:44:44,000 --> 00:44:46,000
is owe the government money every year

113
00:44:46,000 --> 00:44:50,000
because the rule is that if you owe more than 10%

114
00:44:50,000 --> 00:44:52,000
of your tax bill to the government two years in a row,

115
00:44:52,000 --> 00:44:54,000
then there's a fine.

116
00:44:54,000 --> 00:44:55,000
So you don't want that.

117
00:44:55,000 --> 00:44:57,000
The fine is noticeable.

118
00:44:57,000 --> 00:45:00,000
But basically, the smartest thing is actually

119
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:02,000
to alternate back and forth

120
00:45:02,000 --> 00:45:04,000
between owing the government a ton of money

121
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:08,000
and only underpaying and only owing them 9% of the bill.

122
00:45:08,000 --> 00:45:11,000
And you just want to oscillate back and forth between that.

123
00:45:11,000 --> 00:45:12,000
That's the idea.

124
00:45:12,000 --> 00:45:14,000
And that way, the government is always giving you

125
00:45:14,000 --> 00:45:17,000
essentially an interest-free loan every year,

126
00:45:17,000 --> 00:45:19,000
varying between a ton of money,

127
00:45:19,000 --> 00:45:22,000
basically you can just pay them the very bare minimum.

128
00:45:22,000 --> 00:45:24,000
The government won't allow you to pay nothing.

129
00:45:24,000 --> 00:45:27,000
You can't go and indicate that you want negative withholding

130
00:45:27,000 --> 00:45:29,000
or something like that, so that's not allowed.

131
00:45:29,000 --> 00:45:31,000
You can have a very low amount of withheld,

132
00:45:31,000 --> 00:45:33,000
and then you switch over, and then you go

133
00:45:33,000 --> 00:45:37,000
and you want to withhold more so you don't get fined.

134
00:45:37,000 --> 00:45:39,000
But you always want to owe the money.

135
00:45:39,000 --> 00:45:41,000
This is basic tax theory.

136
00:45:41,000 --> 00:45:45,000
But again, most people would be horrified of this idea.

137
00:45:45,000 --> 00:45:46,000
But I don't want to be one of you

138
00:45:46,000 --> 00:45:48,000
writing a big check at the end of the year.

139
00:45:48,000 --> 00:45:51,000
But if you do it this way, you will have all the money

140
00:45:51,000 --> 00:45:55,000
you need to write the check plus some interest.

141
00:45:55,000 --> 00:45:56,000
Write a negative.

142
00:45:56,000 --> 00:45:58,000
Remember, the government gives you 0%,

143
00:45:58,000 --> 00:46:01,000
not a little interest.

144
00:46:01,000 --> 00:46:03,000
You can do better than that.

145
00:46:03,000 --> 00:46:08,000
So weird, but that's how people seem to be.

146
00:46:08,000 --> 00:46:10,000
Other cases, people seem to have discount rates

147
00:46:10,000 --> 00:46:12,000
and excess interest rate.

148
00:46:12,000 --> 00:46:13,000
So there's at least some evidence

149
00:46:13,000 --> 00:46:16,000
that when people buy major appliances,

150
00:46:16,000 --> 00:46:19,000
they don't put as much weight as they should

151
00:46:19,000 --> 00:46:22,000
on future energy savings.

152
00:46:22,000 --> 00:46:25,000
So that one is a little bit harder.

153
00:46:25,000 --> 00:46:28,000
And actually, yes.

154
00:46:28,000 --> 00:46:30,000
This last week, a decision was made

155
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:32,000
that we needed to replace all of our windows.

156
00:46:32,000 --> 00:46:34,000
And this was after I said,

157
00:46:34,000 --> 00:46:36,000
okay, well I would do it if the energy savings

158
00:46:36,000 --> 00:46:39,000
according to neutral sources were actually substantial.

159
00:46:39,000 --> 00:46:40,000
And then I looked it up,

160
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:42,000
and since we already have double-tane windows,

161
00:46:42,000 --> 00:46:44,000
the windows are not actually much better.

162
00:46:44,000 --> 00:46:47,000
But I was vetoed.

163
00:46:47,000 --> 00:46:49,000
So we are getting some way or so,

164
00:46:49,000 --> 00:46:51,000
like almost all our windows replaced

165
00:46:51,000 --> 00:46:53,000
at enormous cost, and it's a poor investment.

166
00:46:53,000 --> 00:46:55,000
But it's a good investment in my future.

167
00:46:55,000 --> 00:46:59,000
So, all right.

168
00:46:59,000 --> 00:47:03,000
So, of course you can always have a strategic theory

169
00:47:03,000 --> 00:47:05,000
if you have a couple where one is doing it

170
00:47:05,000 --> 00:47:06,000
because the other one wants to do it.

171
00:47:06,000 --> 00:47:07,000
But then there's a question,

172
00:47:07,000 --> 00:47:09,000
well why does the other one want to do it?

173
00:47:09,000 --> 00:47:10,000
Okay.

174
00:47:10,000 --> 00:47:11,000
All right, and then furthermore,

175
00:47:11,000 --> 00:47:12,000
there's some evidence that people have

176
00:47:12,000 --> 00:47:15,000
different discount rates for gains versus losses.

177
00:47:15,000 --> 00:47:18,000
There is a classic experiment involving,

178
00:47:18,000 --> 00:47:20,000
I think these are both hypotheticals,

179
00:47:20,000 --> 00:47:22,000
but still I will say that they do resonate.

180
00:47:22,000 --> 00:47:24,000
So the experiment where they asked people two things,

181
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:26,000
one of them was,

182
00:47:26,000 --> 00:47:28,000
if you either get electroshocked right now

183
00:47:28,000 --> 00:47:29,000
or get electroshocked in a week,

184
00:47:29,000 --> 00:47:31,000
which one would you rather have?

185
00:47:31,000 --> 00:47:35,000
And the normal answer is actually shock me right now.

186
00:47:35,000 --> 00:47:39,000
And again, on the theory that I will be nervous all week.

187
00:47:39,000 --> 00:47:41,000
On the other hand, there's also one saying,

188
00:47:41,000 --> 00:47:43,000
if you could get a kiss from your favorite movie star

189
00:47:43,000 --> 00:47:45,000
right now or in a week.

190
00:47:45,000 --> 00:47:47,000
People generally say in a week

191
00:47:47,000 --> 00:47:48,000
because they want to spend a week

192
00:47:48,000 --> 00:47:50,000
thinking about how great it's going to be.

193
00:47:50,000 --> 00:47:52,000
Just, ah, that could be so cool.

194
00:47:52,000 --> 00:48:00,000
All right, so, all right.

195
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:02,000
Now, so there's that example.

196
00:48:02,000 --> 00:48:05,000
Then there's another striking one.

197
00:48:05,000 --> 00:48:10,000
And this is one which is on the surface less strange,

198
00:48:10,000 --> 00:48:12,000
but it's weird, it gets very weird

199
00:48:12,000 --> 00:48:14,000
the more you think about it.

200
00:48:14,000 --> 00:48:16,000
All right, so discount rates seem to vary

201
00:48:16,000 --> 00:48:20,000
with total waiting time in the sense that they actually,

202
00:48:20,000 --> 00:48:22,000
your discount rate predictably changes

203
00:48:22,000 --> 00:48:25,000
if you let some time pass.

204
00:48:25,000 --> 00:48:30,000
In other words, people will predictably change their minds

205
00:48:30,000 --> 00:48:33,000
if you give them some time.

206
00:48:33,000 --> 00:48:36,000
All right, so the kind of thing where

207
00:48:36,000 --> 00:48:38,000
if someone will predictably say,

208
00:48:38,000 --> 00:48:41,000
I want to get a bathtub without the features,

209
00:48:41,000 --> 00:48:43,000
and then in a year's time you say,

210
00:48:43,000 --> 00:48:46,000
look, very likely that you will regret the choice.

211
00:48:46,000 --> 00:48:48,000
You're saying 90% of people regret the choice

212
00:48:48,000 --> 00:48:50,000
in which they got more features afterwards.

213
00:48:50,000 --> 00:48:52,000
All right, so the classic example is

214
00:48:52,000 --> 00:48:56,000
if you ask someone to, like they say,

215
00:48:56,000 --> 00:48:58,000
look, you can either have something today or tomorrow,

216
00:48:58,000 --> 00:49:00,000
they put a high weight on getting it now.

217
00:49:00,000 --> 00:49:02,000
But if you say you can either have this item

218
00:49:02,000 --> 00:49:06,000
in 365 days or 366 days,

219
00:49:06,000 --> 00:49:08,000
then people see very little difference

220
00:49:08,000 --> 00:49:11,000
between waiting a year or a year and a day.

221
00:49:11,000 --> 00:49:14,000
Now, here's the funny thing about passage of time.

222
00:49:14,000 --> 00:49:16,000
In one year, what will have happened

223
00:49:16,000 --> 00:49:20,000
to that choice of 365 days versus 366 days?

224
00:49:20,000 --> 00:49:23,000
It will have turned into a choice between

225
00:49:23,000 --> 00:49:25,000
today and tomorrow.

226
00:49:25,000 --> 00:49:28,000
And at that point, there is a predictable tendency

227
00:49:28,000 --> 00:49:30,000
for people to want to go and pay some money

228
00:49:30,000 --> 00:49:33,000
to go and get rid of the last day's weight,

229
00:49:33,000 --> 00:49:35,000
a weight that they were perfectly happy

230
00:49:35,000 --> 00:49:39,000
to accept one year earlier.

231
00:49:39,000 --> 00:49:43,000
All right, so this implies what is sometimes

232
00:49:43,000 --> 00:49:50,000
called a dynamic inconsistency.

233
00:49:50,000 --> 00:49:52,000
It's tempting to say this shows that people are transitive,

234
00:49:52,000 --> 00:49:53,000
but it's actually stronger.

235
00:49:53,000 --> 00:49:55,000
People are irreflexive.

236
00:49:55,000 --> 00:49:59,000
Irreflexive, so remember the reflexive property preferences.

237
00:49:59,000 --> 00:50:04,000
If A is preferred to B, then B is not preferred to A.

238
00:50:04,000 --> 00:50:06,000
Right? Pretty obvious.

239
00:50:06,000 --> 00:50:10,000
And yet, this says that the simple passage of time

240
00:50:10,000 --> 00:50:12,000
will, on average, turn people from people

241
00:50:12,000 --> 00:50:17,000
who prefer not weight over weighting

242
00:50:17,000 --> 00:50:22,000
into people who prefer not weighting.

243
00:50:22,000 --> 00:50:24,000
So, like, if it's, you can either see the new

244
00:50:24,000 --> 00:50:26,000
Star Wars movie in a year or a year and a day,

245
00:50:26,000 --> 00:50:29,000
and you have to pay 10% more to see it in one year,

246
00:50:29,000 --> 00:50:31,000
fine, I'll go see it. What's the big deal?

247
00:50:31,000 --> 00:50:32,000
It's one extra day.

248
00:50:32,000 --> 00:50:34,000
But the day the movie comes out,

249
00:50:34,000 --> 00:50:37,000
oh my God, I would be happy to go and not only

250
00:50:37,000 --> 00:50:39,000
give those savings, I'll pay an extra premium

251
00:50:39,000 --> 00:50:41,000
so I can see it right now.

252
00:50:41,000 --> 00:50:43,000
I don't want to wait now.

253
00:50:43,000 --> 00:50:44,000
Okay.

254
00:50:44,000 --> 00:50:48,000
Now, this is often seen as a self-control problem.

255
00:50:48,000 --> 00:50:51,000
Often seen as a self-control problem.

256
00:50:51,000 --> 00:50:55,000
The idea that when you have a long time to wait,

257
00:50:55,000 --> 00:50:58,000
then your emotions are not very strong,

258
00:50:58,000 --> 00:51:01,000
and then you can go and do the wise, prudent thing.

259
00:51:01,000 --> 00:51:03,000
Worse, if it's like, come on, you've got to make a decision right now!

260
00:51:03,000 --> 00:51:04,000
Choose, choose, choose!

261
00:51:04,000 --> 00:51:09,000
That's where people get excited, and then, no!

262
00:51:09,000 --> 00:51:12,000
Alright, something like that.

263
00:51:12,000 --> 00:51:17,000
Alright, so, we have many examples of these alleged self-control problems.

264
00:51:17,000 --> 00:51:20,000
One, of course, is if you ask people,

265
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:23,000
do you want to lose weight this year, or stay your current weight?

266
00:51:23,000 --> 00:51:25,000
Well, maybe even gain some weight.

267
00:51:25,000 --> 00:51:28,000
Like imagine, do you want to stay your current weight, or gain weight?

268
00:51:28,000 --> 00:51:30,000
Very few people on January 1st will say,

269
00:51:30,000 --> 00:51:32,000
I think this will be a weight-gaining year.

270
00:51:32,000 --> 00:51:35,000
Sounds like an optimal pattern.

271
00:51:35,000 --> 00:51:39,000
And yet, of course, a lot of people do gain weight in a given year.

272
00:51:39,000 --> 00:51:42,000
I think the average person gains about a pound a year.

273
00:51:42,000 --> 00:51:46,000
So, people wind up doing something,

274
00:51:46,000 --> 00:51:50,000
which, of course, how is it you gain weight?

275
00:51:50,000 --> 00:51:54,000
Well, nobody actually goes and chooses an eating path for a year.

276
00:51:54,000 --> 00:51:58,000
Rather, at any given meal, you've got the choice of eating more or less.

277
00:51:58,000 --> 00:52:02,000
In fact, between meals, you have a choice of creating a new meal.

278
00:52:02,000 --> 00:52:04,000
How many meals should I have today?

279
00:52:04,000 --> 00:52:06,000
How many snacks?

280
00:52:06,000 --> 00:52:08,000
When I go to grocery stores now,

281
00:52:08,000 --> 00:52:10,000
and I just remember how thin I was,

282
00:52:10,000 --> 00:52:13,000
and how hard it was for me to gain weight when I was a kid,

283
00:52:13,000 --> 00:52:17,000
and how stingy my parents were with anything that tasted good back then,

284
00:52:17,000 --> 00:52:20,000
I was like, why couldn't I have had an unlimited amount of money

285
00:52:20,000 --> 00:52:23,000
to eat delicious stuff when I didn't gain weight?

286
00:52:23,000 --> 00:52:26,000
Because it's so good, this food tastes so wonderful.

287
00:52:26,000 --> 00:52:30,000
And now, when I can fill my basket with fantastic food all the time,

288
00:52:30,000 --> 00:52:34,000
I would be fat as a house if I actually did what is in my heart,

289
00:52:34,000 --> 00:52:38,000
which is just like, ice cream, three bowls of ice cream every day,

290
00:52:38,000 --> 00:52:40,000
sundaes, sundaes are so wonderful,

291
00:52:40,000 --> 00:52:43,000
it's fantastic, endless candy of every variety,

292
00:52:43,000 --> 00:52:45,000
I want to try them all.

293
00:52:45,000 --> 00:52:47,000
But I can't do that now.

294
00:52:47,000 --> 00:52:49,000
It would be very bad for me to try now.

295
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:53,000
If there was a time, my parents just would have been not so stingy,

296
00:52:53,000 --> 00:52:55,000
and health conscious,

297
00:52:55,000 --> 00:52:57,000
when I could have had a wonderful experience,

298
00:52:57,000 --> 00:53:00,000
I could be looking back on it and saying, wow, so great being a kid,

299
00:53:00,000 --> 00:53:02,000
pigging out, being thin.

300
00:53:02,000 --> 00:53:05,000
Anyway, some self-control problems.

301
00:53:05,000 --> 00:53:10,000
So there's no one, almost no one says, I want to gain weight this year,

302
00:53:10,000 --> 00:53:14,000
and yet people gain weight by making these very short-run choices

303
00:53:14,000 --> 00:53:16,000
which add up to gaining weight.

304
00:53:16,000 --> 00:53:19,000
People generally don't want to be destitute when they retire,

305
00:53:19,000 --> 00:53:22,000
and yet people, week after week,

306
00:53:22,000 --> 00:53:25,000
will choose not to go and save for their retirement.

307
00:53:25,000 --> 00:53:27,000
Many examples like this.

308
00:53:27,000 --> 00:53:30,000
So you might want to save for retirement, but you have this constant urge to spend.

309
00:53:30,000 --> 00:53:34,000
Of course, there's exercising, so in general, people say,

310
00:53:34,000 --> 00:53:36,000
do I want to exercise this year or not?

311
00:53:36,000 --> 00:53:38,000
Definitely exercise, but then each day,

312
00:53:38,000 --> 00:53:40,000
I don't feel like today,

313
00:53:40,000 --> 00:53:43,000
and I don't feel like tomorrow, and the next day, I don't feel like tomorrow,

314
00:53:43,000 --> 00:53:45,000
and I pretty much don't feel like it ever.

