01:24:19,240 --> 01:24:21,760
And so I'm gonna think that he's less rich.

107
01:24:21,760 --> 01:24:23,400
Right, whereas another person

108
01:24:23,400 --> 01:24:25,080
could be driving the very same car

109
01:24:25,080 --> 01:24:26,200
and you would say, hey,

110
01:24:26,200 --> 01:24:27,640
a graduate student's driving that car,

111
01:24:27,640 --> 01:24:29,840
that graduate student, that made me change my mind,

112
01:24:29,840 --> 01:24:31,520
think that graduate student's really rich.

113
01:24:31,520 --> 01:24:32,360
But if Bill Gates doing it,

114
01:24:32,360 --> 01:24:34,080
it would make you think less of him

115
01:24:34,080 --> 01:24:36,200
because you expect so much.

116
01:24:36,200 --> 01:24:37,040
Yeah, Ash.

117
01:24:37,040 --> 01:24:38,440
If this is saying every day,

118
01:24:38,440 --> 01:24:40,320
I'm more and more convinced that Brian is a jerk,

119
01:24:40,320 --> 01:24:42,240
is that equivalent to saying,

120
01:24:42,240 --> 01:24:44,960
every day Brian surprises me more and more

121
01:24:44,960 --> 01:24:45,880
with how much of a jerk he is?

122
01:24:45,880 --> 01:24:47,400
Yes, yes, yes, exactly.

123
01:24:47,400 --> 01:24:48,680
Every day there's more,

124
01:24:48,680 --> 01:24:50,720
there's another surprise piece of evidence

125
01:24:50,720 --> 01:24:52,160
in favor of jerkiness.

126
01:24:52,160 --> 01:24:54,400
It's like, well, you've been surprised

127
01:24:54,400 --> 01:24:55,520
like a hundred times in a row?

128
01:24:55,520 --> 01:24:56,960
What's wrong with you?

129
01:24:56,960 --> 01:24:58,000
Like, how could you?

130
01:24:58,000 --> 01:24:59,040
I mean, like that's,

131
01:24:59,040 --> 01:25:00,880
makes as much sense as every day

132
01:25:00,880 --> 01:25:03,000
someone stands behind a corner and says boo,

133
01:25:03,000 --> 01:25:05,440
and every day you're like, ah, ah,

134
01:25:05,440 --> 01:25:06,400
a hundred times in a row.

135
01:25:06,400 --> 01:25:08,280
It's like, eventually it should get old.

136
01:25:08,280 --> 01:25:10,080
Like, well, you always do that.

137
01:25:10,080 --> 01:25:11,600
It's not surprising at all anymore.

138
01:25:11,600 --> 01:25:13,840
Not scary at all anymore.

139
01:25:13,840 --> 01:25:14,680
That's the idea.

140
01:25:15,840 --> 01:25:16,920
All right, so anyway,

141
01:25:16,920 --> 01:25:18,400
Bayesian framework's about updating

142
01:25:18,400 --> 01:25:19,880
whenever someone says,

143
01:25:19,880 --> 01:25:21,800
every day I become more convinced.

144
01:25:21,800 --> 01:25:22,960
That is a strong sign

145
01:25:22,960 --> 01:25:24,400
that you should just ignore what that person says

146
01:25:24,400 --> 01:25:25,920
because they're a bad Bayesian.

147
01:25:26,760 --> 01:25:27,600
Right?

148
01:25:27,600 --> 01:25:29,120
Normally people say it as a brag.

149
01:25:29,120 --> 01:25:31,560
It's like, I'm just so right all the time.

150
01:25:31,560 --> 01:25:34,240
Everything keeps confirming what I thought.

151
01:25:34,240 --> 01:25:35,760
It's like, well, essentially,

152
01:25:35,760 --> 01:25:37,720
in other words, what you're saying is that

153
01:25:37,720 --> 01:25:41,040
every day you fail to adequately adjust your belief

154
01:25:41,040 --> 01:25:42,440
in response to information

155
01:25:42,440 --> 01:25:44,160
so you can be surprised over and over again.

156
01:25:44,160 --> 01:25:45,720
But let's go and listen to people

157
01:25:45,720 --> 01:25:48,600
who every day things are either,

158
01:25:48,600 --> 01:25:50,680
they're equally likely to move their mind

159
01:25:50,680 --> 01:25:51,680
in either direction.

160
01:25:52,640 --> 01:25:53,880
You know, generally, anytime someone says

161
01:25:53,880 --> 01:25:57,520
I've never seen any evidence that was against this,

162
01:25:57,520 --> 01:25:59,240
it's like, never, ever.

163
01:26:00,480 --> 01:26:04,240
Funny, I'm seeing evidence against my views all the time.

164
01:26:04,240 --> 01:26:06,840
Because given, like, once my view is confident,

165
01:26:06,840 --> 01:26:09,280
any little error, it's like, hmm,

166
01:26:09,280 --> 01:26:11,800
that's not what you'd expect if this theory is right.

167
01:26:11,800 --> 01:26:13,440
Even if it's a small deviation.

168
01:26:13,440 --> 01:26:14,280
Strange.

169
01:26:15,400 --> 01:26:16,240
All right, so anyway,

170
01:26:16,240 --> 01:26:18,520
Bayesian framework's all about updating.

171
01:26:18,520 --> 01:26:19,760
But there's a bunch of experiments showing

172
01:26:19,760 --> 01:26:21,640
what's called belief perseverance,

173
01:26:21,640 --> 01:26:23,400
where people stick with their initial view

174
01:26:23,400 --> 01:26:25,680
even when contrary evidence pours in.

175
01:26:26,520 --> 01:26:27,360
All right.

176
01:26:27,360 --> 01:26:29,960
And there is a, you know,

177
01:26:29,960 --> 01:26:32,360
there is a set of experiments finding that people

178
01:26:32,360 --> 01:26:34,960
can actually be more accurate with less information.

179
01:26:35,840 --> 01:26:37,920
All right, now this is kind of a weird experiment,

180
01:26:37,920 --> 01:26:39,960
but it's still pretty famous

181
01:26:39,960 --> 01:26:40,880
and people are talking about it,

182
01:26:40,880 --> 01:26:42,320
so I'll share it with you.

183
01:26:42,320 --> 01:26:44,920
So this is an experiment where

184
01:26:44,920 --> 01:26:47,880
you either show someone a blurry image

185
01:26:47,880 --> 01:26:50,080
and then you gradually make it clearer,

186
01:26:51,040 --> 01:26:54,640
or you just show them the last frame of the series.

187
01:26:55,680 --> 01:26:57,400
So in other words, you know, you only get,

188
01:26:57,400 --> 01:26:58,640
so in both cases,

189
01:26:58,640 --> 01:27:01,040
so imagine that you see 30 pictures,

190
01:27:01,040 --> 01:27:03,400
you know, 30 pictures of the picture,

191
01:27:03,400 --> 01:27:06,400
30 screenshots of the picture

192
01:27:06,400 --> 01:27:08,360
getting more, less and less blurry,

193
01:27:08,360 --> 01:27:09,880
although the last picture's still kind of blurry,

194
01:27:09,880 --> 01:27:11,040
that's key.

195
01:27:11,040 --> 01:27:13,040
Or you would just see picture number 30.

196
01:27:14,200 --> 01:27:15,400
And in these experiments,

197
01:27:15,400 --> 01:27:18,160
it seems like people only see picture number 30,

198
01:27:18,160 --> 01:27:20,680
are better able to guess what the picture really shows

199
01:27:20,680 --> 01:27:23,120
than people who saw a whole series of pictures

200
01:27:23,120 --> 01:27:25,840
of the thing gradually coming into focus.

201
01:27:25,840 --> 01:27:27,640
And the usual story is,

202
01:27:27,640 --> 01:27:29,600
the people who saw 30 pictures

203
01:27:29,600 --> 01:27:32,920
formed a theory about what they were seeing early on,

204
01:27:32,920 --> 01:27:34,480
and then once they formed the theory,

205
01:27:34,480 --> 01:27:35,400
then every picture's like,

206
01:27:35,400 --> 01:27:37,760
oh yeah, that's the nose of the penguin.

207
01:27:37,760 --> 01:27:39,520
Oh yeah, yeah, yeah, that's his wing.

208
01:27:39,520 --> 01:27:42,400
And each one, they are imposing their theory

209
01:27:42,400 --> 01:27:43,680
upon the evidence.

210
01:27:43,680 --> 01:27:45,320
Whereas if you only see the last one,

211
01:27:45,320 --> 01:27:47,800
then you don't have a chance

212
01:27:47,800 --> 01:27:49,680
to go and fit everything into this

213
01:27:49,680 --> 01:27:51,000
emerging body of information,

214
01:27:51,000 --> 01:27:53,040
then you just look at the last

215
01:27:53,040 --> 01:27:55,080
and of course most informative picture

216
01:27:55,080 --> 01:27:56,560
and form your belief on that.

217
01:27:57,480 --> 01:27:59,680
So that is one experiment.

218
01:27:59,680 --> 01:28:01,560
Now, important to remember that in the experiment,

219
01:28:01,560 --> 01:28:03,200
they never show you a clear picture.

220
01:28:03,200 --> 01:28:05,360
It goes from being super blurry

221
01:28:05,360 --> 01:28:07,200
to kind of blurry.

222
01:28:07,200 --> 01:28:08,800
If it eventually was crystal clear,

223
01:28:08,800 --> 01:28:11,240
then this result almost certainly wouldn't work.

224
01:28:11,240 --> 01:28:12,080
Right?

225
01:28:12,080 --> 01:28:14,040
If in the end, it's like the Joker for Batman

226
01:28:14,040 --> 01:28:16,720
and you thought it was a bird,

227
01:28:16,720 --> 01:28:19,440
the actual penguin, the bird, not the super villain,

228
01:28:19,440 --> 01:28:22,200
then you'll, I think, be very unlikely

229
01:28:22,200 --> 01:28:23,880
that someone would make that mistake.

230
01:28:23,880 --> 01:28:25,800
But if you keep them,

231
01:28:25,800 --> 01:28:27,360
if they're blurry before and afterwards,

232
01:28:27,360 --> 01:28:28,760
but you give people a chance

233
01:28:28,760 --> 01:28:30,880
to start getting their mental gears turning

234
01:28:30,880 --> 01:28:33,680
and thinking, oh yeah, that's kind of what it is,

235
01:28:33,680 --> 01:28:35,680
that's where the problem emerges.

236
01:28:35,680 --> 01:28:37,280
Yes, is there a question there?

237
01:28:39,600 --> 01:28:40,880
All right.

238
01:28:40,880 --> 01:28:43,560
Okay, now other experiments find a stronger effect.

239
01:28:43,560 --> 01:28:44,880
Once you believe in hypothesis,

240
01:28:44,880 --> 01:28:47,000
then they get increasingly confident.

241
01:28:47,000 --> 01:28:48,280
Right, per the saying.

242
01:28:48,280 --> 01:28:50,440
Every day, the evidence is mounting.

243
01:28:50,440 --> 01:28:52,480
Like more and more convinced every day.

244
01:28:53,880 --> 01:28:54,840
It's like, wow.

245
01:28:54,840 --> 01:28:57,680
So how agnostic were you at the beginning

246
01:28:57,680 --> 01:28:59,360
if you've spent the last 30 years

247
01:28:59,360 --> 01:29:03,160
becoming more and more convinced every single day?

248
01:29:03,160 --> 01:29:06,720
Wow, it's like, seems like you'd be

249
01:29:06,720 --> 01:29:09,200
quite agnostic initially.

250
01:29:09,200 --> 01:29:10,200
All right.

251
01:29:10,200 --> 01:29:11,400
So anyway, the story here is that

252
01:29:11,400 --> 01:29:13,400
more likely to notice confirming evidence

253
01:29:13,400 --> 01:29:15,240
and to misinterpret ambiguous evidence

254
01:29:15,240 --> 01:29:16,440
as additional support.

255
01:29:16,440 --> 01:29:17,480
And this has a special name.

256
01:29:17,480 --> 01:29:20,040
It's called confirmatory bias or confirmation bias.

257
01:29:26,560 --> 01:29:29,000
All right, so, simple example.

258
01:29:29,000 --> 01:29:31,840
So yeah, going back to the Brian is a jerk story.

259
01:29:31,840 --> 01:29:33,320
Once you believe this story,

260
01:29:33,320 --> 01:29:36,280
then you might just sort of follow me around

261
01:29:36,280 --> 01:29:38,800
and look for examples of me being a jerk.

262
01:29:38,800 --> 01:29:41,360
And then you say, look, the evidence is overwhelming.

263
01:29:41,360 --> 01:29:44,080
I've got 30 different instances.

264
01:29:44,080 --> 01:29:47,080
Right, but that's not a very good way to test the theory.

265
01:29:47,080 --> 01:29:49,120
Right, it's one thing you should be doing,

266
01:29:49,120 --> 01:29:50,480
but what should you also be doing

267
01:29:50,480 --> 01:29:51,880
in order to test the theory?

268
01:29:53,000 --> 01:29:56,480
Yes, making a list of all the times I'm not a jerk

269
01:29:56,480 --> 01:29:58,720
and looking for disconfirmatory evidence.

270
01:29:58,720 --> 01:30:00,080
And then once you've got all that,

271
01:30:00,080 --> 01:30:02,040
you say, well, what would the ratio be

272
01:30:02,040 --> 01:30:03,640
for a jerk versus an un-jerk?

273
01:30:04,680 --> 01:30:05,520
Right, and then it might be,

274
01:30:05,520 --> 01:30:08,520
oh, actually, this is totally expected.

275
01:30:08,520 --> 01:30:10,800
Right, you know, just like someone might,

276
01:30:10,800 --> 01:30:13,440
so a communist might look at the American economy

277
01:30:13,440 --> 01:30:14,720
and then go and make a list

278
01:30:14,720 --> 01:30:17,000
of a thousand horrible things about it

279
01:30:17,000 --> 01:30:19,600
and then say, communism is true, obviously.

280
01:30:19,600 --> 01:30:21,400
Who can deny the facts?

281
01:30:21,400 --> 01:30:24,640
It's like, well, all you did was go to a world

282
01:30:24,640 --> 01:30:26,920
or a country of 300 million people

283
01:30:26,920 --> 01:30:28,920
and go and find a thousand problems,

284
01:30:28,920 --> 01:30:30,760
which can't be that hard, actually,

285
01:30:30,760 --> 01:30:32,520
and then say, you're right,

286
01:30:32,520 --> 01:30:34,960
that's a terrible way of approaching it.

287
01:30:34,960 --> 01:30:36,040
You'd want it, like, again,

288
01:30:36,040 --> 01:30:38,840
you could go and list all the bad things

289
01:30:38,840 --> 01:30:40,800
and then list all the good things.

290
01:30:40,800 --> 01:30:42,200
You could also do random sampling.

291
01:30:42,200 --> 01:30:43,600
That's another nice approach.

292
01:30:43,600 --> 01:30:45,800
Just say, look, I'm gonna go and take a look every minute,

293
01:30:45,800 --> 01:30:47,600
and I'm just gonna go and look at a random news article

294
01:30:47,600 --> 01:30:49,920
and see what's going on.

295
01:30:49,920 --> 01:30:52,520
Why didn't that would be a much better way of doing it, too?

296
01:30:52,520 --> 01:30:54,840
Right, there's many better ways of doing it

297
01:30:54,840 --> 01:30:56,480
than just going and starting with a theory

298
01:30:56,480 --> 01:30:58,560
and then looking around for every story

299
01:30:58,560 --> 01:30:59,880
that advances it.

300
01:30:59,880 --> 01:31:04,840
This is the reasonable core of Popperian falsificationism,

301
01:31:04,840 --> 01:31:07,080
where he says, yes, give it,

302
01:31:07,080 --> 01:31:08,680
but I'm not sure whether he said

303
01:31:08,680 --> 01:31:10,440
whether he actually framed it in terms of psychology.

304
01:31:10,440 --> 01:31:12,840
At least his followers have never done so to me,

305
01:31:12,840 --> 01:31:15,760
but the idea is if there's a theory you think is right,

306
01:31:15,760 --> 01:31:18,600
look around for all the reasons that it's wrong.

307
01:31:18,600 --> 01:31:21,600
You'll be very energetic in trying to find reasons

308
01:31:21,600 --> 01:31:22,800
to change your mind about something.

309
01:31:22,800 --> 01:31:25,520
We'll be looking for exceptions, right?

310
01:31:25,520 --> 01:31:28,800
Like if you think that the economy is verbally competitive,

311
01:31:28,800 --> 01:31:31,680
you might just spend a day walking around looking at verbs

312
01:31:31,680 --> 01:31:33,960
and try to find things that look like they don't fit

313
01:31:33,960 --> 01:31:36,240
with a verbally competitive model.

314
01:31:36,240 --> 01:31:39,560
Like go to a movie theater at the matinee,

315
01:31:39,560 --> 01:31:42,560
and you will see an overwhelming contradiction

316
01:31:42,560 --> 01:31:45,000
to the verbally competitive model.

317
01:31:45,000 --> 01:31:50,000
Whoa, 90% of the seats are empty, but the price was $8,

318
01:31:50,120 --> 01:31:55,120
and the marginal cost appears to be maybe eight cents tops.

319
01:31:55,120 --> 01:31:57,560
So the verbally competitive model says the price will,

320
01:31:57,560 --> 01:31:59,200
the price, the marginal cost is eight cents.

321
01:31:59,200 --> 01:32:01,160
Verbally competitive model says the price will be?

322
01:32:01,160 --> 01:32:02,280
Eight cents.

323
01:32:02,280 --> 01:32:03,320
Eight cents.

324
01:32:03,320 --> 01:32:05,800
It's $8, how can that be?

325
01:32:05,800 --> 01:32:08,360
Right, so these are the kinds of things to be looking for,

326
01:32:08,360 --> 01:32:10,200
or if you're looking for,

327
01:32:10,200 --> 01:32:13,560
so you know, for price differences, try to explain them.

328
01:32:13,560 --> 01:32:15,520
Right, so verbally competitive model says

329
01:32:15,520 --> 01:32:16,880
the different products can have different prices,

330
01:32:16,880 --> 01:32:19,280
but only if the costs are different.

331
01:32:20,240 --> 01:32:22,960
Right, and then you might even go and look at things

332
01:32:22,960 --> 01:32:24,520
that initially seem to support the model and say,

333
01:32:24,520 --> 01:32:26,880
wait a second, actually they don't really support the model.

334
01:32:26,880 --> 01:32:29,400
Like think about this, every flavor of ice cream

335
01:32:29,400 --> 01:32:32,160
is the same price within a brand normally.

336
01:32:32,160 --> 01:32:33,880
What are the odds that the cost of producing

337
01:32:33,880 --> 01:32:35,120
every one of those flavors of ice cream

338
01:32:35,120 --> 01:32:37,360
is exactly equal to the penny?

339
01:32:39,440 --> 01:32:41,120
Seems hard to believe.

340
01:32:41,120 --> 01:32:41,960
Yeah, it seems to me.

341
01:32:41,960 --> 01:32:43,920
So again, like the only theory that I know that makes sense

342
01:32:43,920 --> 01:32:46,040
is that they vary the content,

343
01:32:46,040 --> 01:32:47,640
the quantity of the expensive ingredients

344
01:32:47,640 --> 01:32:50,840
to hit a certain price, but again, that's,

345
01:32:50,840 --> 01:32:52,520
so like they're telling me that the cost of mint

346
01:32:52,520 --> 01:32:55,760
is exactly equal to the cost of chocolate syrup.

347
01:32:55,760 --> 01:32:57,720
Like, that's weird.

348
01:32:57,720 --> 01:33:00,000
Like what a world that these things would all be,

349
01:33:00,000 --> 01:33:02,560
so it would all be equal in price.

350
01:33:02,560 --> 01:33:04,920
So there's many opportunities to go and look

351
01:33:04,920 --> 01:33:06,880
for these counter examples.

352
01:33:06,880 --> 01:33:09,880
So it's looking for the model to fail in interesting ways.

353
01:33:09,880 --> 01:33:11,640
Yeah, yeah, there's lots and lots and lots

354
01:33:11,640 --> 01:33:12,480
of interesting ways to fail.

355
01:33:12,480 --> 01:33:14,960
And then of course, the fact that you've found

356
01:33:14,960 --> 01:33:16,240
what appears to be a counter example,

357
01:33:16,240 --> 01:33:18,880
it might be like on third, on second thought,

358
01:33:18,880 --> 01:33:20,120
you say counter example, third thought,

359
01:33:20,120 --> 01:33:21,560
maybe it's not really a good counter example

360
01:33:21,560 --> 01:33:22,840
to think about it more.

361
01:33:22,840 --> 01:33:24,480
Anyway, these are standard tactics

362
01:33:24,480 --> 01:33:27,800
for avoiding this confirmation bias.

363
01:33:27,800 --> 01:33:29,520
All right, now in terms of experiments

364
01:33:29,520 --> 01:33:32,320
on confirmation bias, my very favorite one in the world

365
01:33:32,320 --> 01:33:36,520
involves a real issue where if we were doing this room,

366
01:33:36,520 --> 01:33:38,120
I would first of all go and find out, right,

367
01:33:38,120 --> 01:33:39,840
so who supports the death penalty, who opposes it?

368
01:33:39,840 --> 01:33:42,520
Great, all right, then I would go and randomly assign

369
01:33:42,520 --> 01:33:45,760
equal numbers of each group to two different rooms.

370
01:33:45,760 --> 01:33:49,480
In room one, I give you two kinds of econometric studies.

371
01:33:49,480 --> 01:33:52,640
One has panel data that shows the death penalty to hers.

372
01:33:52,640 --> 01:33:54,360
Another has cross-sectional data

373
01:33:54,360 --> 01:33:56,440
showing that it doesn't deter.

374
01:33:56,440 --> 01:33:59,000
The other room has panel data showing

375
01:33:59,000 --> 01:34:00,520
that it's penalty does not deter

376
01:34:00,520 --> 01:34:03,320
and cross-sectional evidence showing that it does.

377
01:34:03,320 --> 01:34:05,480
What happens when you do this experiment?

378
01:34:05,480 --> 01:34:08,820
Everyone gets more confident in their view, everyone.

379
01:34:08,820 --> 01:34:11,160
All four groups, right?

380
01:34:11,160 --> 01:34:13,260
And how does this happen?

381
01:34:13,260 --> 01:34:16,840
Well, in the room where the panel data is pro-death penalty,

382
01:34:16,840 --> 01:34:19,520
what do the pro-death penalty people say?

383
01:34:19,520 --> 01:34:20,340
Of course.

384
01:34:20,340 --> 01:34:23,160
Yeah, panel data is the rival data, cross-sectional,

385
01:34:23,160 --> 01:34:25,520
ugh, all right, and of course,

386
01:34:25,520 --> 01:34:28,080
the people that don't like the death penalty in that room

387
01:34:28,080 --> 01:34:30,200
reverse their econometric judgment.

388
01:34:30,200 --> 01:34:32,800
Cross-sectional is so much more probative than panel data.

389
01:34:32,800 --> 01:34:34,240
You can do anything with panel data.

390
01:34:34,240 --> 01:34:35,920
Meanwhile, over in the other room,

391
01:34:35,920 --> 01:34:39,680
your counterpart who has the same prejudice that you do

392
01:34:39,680 --> 01:34:42,080
but received reversed information

393
01:34:42,080 --> 01:34:44,200
is rationalizing the very same view

394
01:34:44,200 --> 01:34:46,600
based upon the methodological superiority

395
01:34:46,600 --> 01:34:48,100
of the opposite kind of study.

396
01:34:49,280 --> 01:34:51,000
So, pretty striking example.

397
01:34:53,000 --> 01:34:55,960
Okay, so questions about that?

398
01:34:55,960 --> 01:34:56,960
So, that one's fine.

399
01:34:58,400 --> 01:35:02,280
All right, and this is something where they go like,

400
01:35:02,280 --> 01:35:05,200
more and more evidence like this has come out.

401
01:35:05,200 --> 01:35:07,000
It's fine to say that more and more evidence has come out.

402
01:35:07,000 --> 01:35:10,420
Not fine to say that I keep getting more convinced, right?

403
01:35:10,420 --> 01:35:12,320
Because to keep getting more convinced,

404
01:35:12,320 --> 01:35:14,960
the evidence would have to come out to a degree of,

405
01:35:14,960 --> 01:35:17,280
it would keep coming out to a greater degree than expected

406
01:35:17,280 --> 01:35:19,520
given how much evidence we've already got, right?

407
01:35:19,520 --> 01:35:21,280
Because if there's a whole bunch of great studies

408
01:35:21,280 --> 01:35:22,400
showing something,

409
01:35:22,400 --> 01:35:24,720
what should you expect to happen in the future?

410
01:35:25,720 --> 01:35:28,920
More great studies that keep saying the same thing, right?

411
01:35:28,920 --> 01:35:30,440
So, when those studies come out,

412
01:35:30,440 --> 01:35:32,480
that's where you say, yeah, pretty much what we had expected

413
01:35:32,480 --> 01:35:34,820
given how solidly established this was.

414
01:35:34,820 --> 01:35:38,900
And you can even have studies that are quite supportive

415
01:35:38,900 --> 01:35:41,400
but not quite as supportive as you thought they would be

416
01:35:41,400 --> 01:35:44,680
leading you to lose some of your confidence.

417
01:35:44,680 --> 01:35:47,000
And again, if you've ever said things like this,

418
01:35:47,000 --> 01:35:49,120
saying, look, I thought, well, the evidence is strong,

419
01:35:49,120 --> 01:35:51,600
it's not as strong as I expected it to be at this point,

420
01:35:51,600 --> 01:35:54,480
and so I've reduced my confidence in the theory,

421
01:35:54,480 --> 01:35:57,380
which is a very weird thing for a human being to say.

422
01:35:57,380 --> 01:35:58,360
But if you've heard someone like,

423
01:35:58,360 --> 01:36:00,200
if that kind of thinking is occurring to you,

424
01:36:00,200 --> 01:36:04,600
you are really internalizing Bayesianism as a way of life

425
01:36:04,600 --> 01:36:06,720
rather than just an equation,

426
01:36:06,720 --> 01:36:09,680
because what goes to the equation if people don't live it?

427
01:36:12,200 --> 01:36:13,680
All right.

428
01:36:13,680 --> 01:36:15,400
All right, now, more general terms.

429
01:36:15,400 --> 01:36:18,360
There is some evidence of systematic overconfidence.

430
01:36:19,360 --> 01:36:21,280
How do you test for overconfidence?

431
01:36:21,280 --> 01:36:24,600
Anyone here read the blogger Scott Alexander?

432
01:36:24,600 --> 01:36:27,880
Yeah, so, every year Scott makes a bunch of predictions

433
01:36:27,880 --> 01:36:30,120
with probabilities, and then at the end of the year,

434
01:36:30,120 --> 01:36:34,160
he goes and he graphs the subject of probabilities

435
01:36:34,160 --> 01:36:35,580
he had at the beginning of the year

436
01:36:35,580 --> 01:36:38,640
with the object of probabilities of what really happens.

437
01:36:38,640 --> 01:36:40,720
Right, and how does that work?

438
01:36:40,720 --> 01:36:44,440
Well, so, when people do this,

439
01:36:44,440 --> 01:36:46,760
they normally code everything

440
01:36:46,760 --> 01:36:49,800
so that everything is at least a 50% probability.

441
01:36:49,800 --> 01:36:52,960
Otherwise, you should just believe the opposite thing.

442
01:36:52,960 --> 01:36:54,920
So anyway, so the normal thing here

443
01:36:54,920 --> 01:36:58,320
is you've got a graph, and it starts at 0.5,

444
01:36:58,320 --> 01:37:02,040
goes to one, and over here, 0.5, also goes to one,

445
01:37:02,040 --> 01:37:04,080
and here are your subject of probabilities

446
01:37:06,800 --> 01:37:09,480
before you actually know what happened.

447
01:37:09,480 --> 01:37:11,480
And here are the object of probabilities

448
01:37:12,440 --> 01:37:13,960
of what actually occurred,

449
01:37:13,960 --> 01:37:15,720
the frequencies with which they occurred.

450
01:37:15,720 --> 01:37:20,560
Now, if you have neither over nor underconfidence,

451
01:37:20,560 --> 01:37:22,360
this will be a nice 45-degree line,

452
01:37:23,680 --> 01:37:26,560
where half the stuff that you assign

453
01:37:26,560 --> 01:37:29,320
a 50% probability to actually occurs,

454
01:37:29,320 --> 01:37:32,240
where 60% of the stuff that you assign

455
01:37:32,240 --> 01:37:34,640
a 60% probability to occurs,

456
01:37:34,640 --> 01:37:36,000
70% of the stuff that you assign

457
01:37:36,000 --> 01:37:38,240
a 70% probability to actually occurs,

458
01:37:38,240 --> 01:37:39,760
100% of the stuff that you assign

459
01:37:39,760 --> 01:37:41,880
a 100% probability to occurs.

460
01:37:41,880 --> 01:37:45,480
That is what's called perfect calibration,

461
01:37:45,480 --> 01:37:48,240
where you are neither over nor underconfidence.

462
01:37:49,640 --> 01:37:52,560
On the other hand, if your actual line,

463
01:37:52,560 --> 01:37:54,480
this 45-degree line right up there,

464
01:37:54,480 --> 01:37:57,800
if your actual confidence looks like that,

465
01:37:57,800 --> 01:38:00,960
remember, you can't have a probability more than one.

466
01:38:00,960 --> 01:38:03,640
I guess if you could fill in something 110%, sure,

467
01:38:03,640 --> 01:38:05,720
but that doesn't make any sense.

468
01:38:05,720 --> 01:38:08,440
Anyway, so if your line looks like that,

469
01:38:08,440 --> 01:38:10,760
are you over or underconfident?

470
01:38:14,280 --> 01:38:17,000
Yeah, so this is where you say that,

471
01:38:17,000 --> 01:38:19,680
so you have a 60% probability that occurs,

472
01:38:19,680 --> 01:38:22,520
and this actually goes and occurs even more often.

473
01:38:23,400 --> 01:38:25,800
Yes, so that's since you can say you're underconfident,

474
01:38:25,800 --> 01:38:28,080
but then you can have this one here, this one here,

475
01:38:28,080 --> 01:38:31,440
that's what we generally see in the real world.

476
01:38:31,440 --> 01:38:33,880
So very typical that if someone says

477
01:38:33,880 --> 01:38:37,360
they're 100% confident, they're right 80% of the time.

478
01:38:37,360 --> 01:38:38,640
100 equals 80.

479
01:38:38,640 --> 01:38:42,440
90 equals 70, so on, so on.

480
01:38:42,440 --> 01:38:44,880
I think for like 50%, I think people actually,

481
01:38:44,880 --> 01:38:47,400
if you say, I can't actually remember

482
01:38:47,400 --> 01:38:50,280
whether even odds, whether you actually are,

483
01:38:50,280 --> 01:38:51,120
whether that means you're actually

484
01:38:51,120 --> 01:38:53,160
right 40% of the time or not, but anyway.

485
01:38:53,160 --> 01:38:55,640
So a lot of evidence of this overconfidence.

486
01:38:58,480 --> 01:39:01,480
All right, a lot of evidence of this overconfidence.

487
01:39:01,480 --> 01:39:06,480
Okay, now, this evidence, however, is not totally solid.

488
01:39:06,800 --> 01:39:08,000
Again, that German psychologist,

489
01:39:08,000 --> 01:39:09,480
Gerhard Gigerenzer, points out,

490
01:39:09,480 --> 01:39:10,600
and this has been acknowledged

491
01:39:10,600 --> 01:39:12,040
by people who've done the regular evidence,

492
01:39:12,040 --> 01:39:14,040
although per confirmation bias,

493
01:39:14,040 --> 01:39:16,680
what do they say about Gigerenzer's evidence?

494
01:39:16,680 --> 01:39:17,520
It's not good enough.

495
01:39:17,520 --> 01:39:19,240
Yeah, yeah, no, no, no, wrong way of doing it,

496
01:39:19,240 --> 01:39:20,320
it doesn't count.

497
01:39:20,320 --> 01:39:22,600
All right, but anyway, so Gigerenzer says

498
01:39:22,600 --> 01:39:26,040
this works if you ask people question by question.

499
01:39:26,040 --> 01:39:29,000
So for example, if you give people an SAT test,

500
01:39:29,000 --> 01:39:30,680
and then for each question you say,

501
01:39:30,680 --> 01:39:31,880
probability you got that one right,

502
01:39:31,880 --> 01:39:32,960
probably you got that one right,

503
01:39:32,960 --> 01:39:35,760
probably you got the other one right, then this works.

504
01:39:35,760 --> 01:39:38,720
But on the other hand, if you ask people the question,

505
01:39:38,720 --> 01:39:40,760
what fraction do you think you got right?

506
01:39:41,880 --> 01:39:43,520
Gigerenzer says, there actually,

507
01:39:43,520 --> 01:39:45,760
there's not much evidence of overconfidence.

508
01:39:45,760 --> 01:39:47,520
So there's something about thinking about it

509
01:39:47,520 --> 01:39:49,680
question by question that where it looks like

510
01:39:49,680 --> 01:39:51,760
your odd people have high overconfidence,

511
01:39:51,760 --> 01:39:54,080
whereas if you ask them in general terms,

512
01:39:54,080 --> 01:39:56,760
like what fraction of other answers will be correct,

513
01:39:56,760 --> 01:39:58,880
then at least there's greatly reduced

514
01:39:58,880 --> 01:40:00,680
evidence of overconfidence.


1
01:40:00,000 --> 01:40:04,000
And which is the one right way to find out whether people are overconfident?

2
01:40:04,000 --> 01:40:06,000
I don't know.

3
01:40:06,000 --> 01:40:08,000
There's two different approaches which give two different answers.

4
01:40:08,000 --> 01:40:11,000
It seems reasonable to me to split the difference and say,

5
01:40:11,000 --> 01:40:14,000
kind of seems like it, but maybe not.

6
01:40:14,000 --> 01:40:18,000
Or, of course, maybe it is highly subject specific.

7
01:40:18,000 --> 01:40:22,000
Like people are very overconfident in their political claims.

8
01:40:22,000 --> 01:40:31,000
But maybe not so overconfident in, say, whether or not they're going to be getting a raise next week or something like that.

9
01:40:31,000 --> 01:40:34,000
Also possible.

10
01:40:34,000 --> 01:40:38,000
Alright, so questions about that?

11
01:40:38,000 --> 01:40:40,000
Yes, Nathan?

12
01:40:40,000 --> 01:40:47,000
Has there been anything on the interaction between this sort of epistemic overconfidence and social desirability bias?

13
01:40:47,000 --> 01:40:49,000
That's a great question, yeah.

14
01:40:49,000 --> 01:40:55,000
It might look really bad to say, oh, well I'm not sure if this thing is a bad thing.

15
01:40:55,000 --> 01:41:00,000
Yeah, I don't know if your dog can be saved by the vet.

16
01:41:00,000 --> 01:41:04,000
I'm sure the vet will be able to take care of this.

17
01:41:04,000 --> 01:41:06,000
That's what a good person says.

18
01:41:06,000 --> 01:41:10,000
An honest person who says, I don't know, he looks incurable to me, I think you're just wasting your money.

19
01:41:10,000 --> 01:41:15,000
Not a nice thing to say.

20
01:41:15,000 --> 01:41:23,000
According to society, although according to true concern for another person,

21
01:41:23,000 --> 01:41:29,000
say, well look, there's a question, can you say the truth with sympathy?

22
01:41:29,000 --> 01:41:37,000
Like, I know a lot about veterinary medicine, there's nothing they can say to your dog, and that person is just taking advantage of you.

23
01:41:37,000 --> 01:41:39,000
Try that.

24
01:41:39,000 --> 01:41:41,000
Might work.

25
01:41:41,000 --> 01:41:45,000
You can't give up on my dog.

26
01:41:45,000 --> 01:41:48,000
Who are you to say whether a dog will live or die?

27
01:41:48,000 --> 01:41:54,000
Doctor of veterinary medicine, qualified as anyone, specializing in this very disease.

28
01:41:54,000 --> 01:41:56,000
No one will get to convince me.

29
01:41:56,000 --> 01:42:03,000
So there's that mentality, which you may have seen in plenty of cases.

30
01:42:03,000 --> 01:42:05,000
So yeah, I don't know if anyone saw that.

31
01:42:05,000 --> 01:42:08,000
It seems very plausible, and maybe that could be a dissertation.

32
01:42:08,000 --> 01:42:09,000
Who knows?

33
01:42:09,000 --> 01:42:13,000
But anyway, so interaction between social desirability bias and overconfidence.

34
01:42:13,000 --> 01:42:22,000
And sort of along those lines, I'd be curious to see what work's been done regarding the sort of general overconfidence,

35
01:42:22,000 --> 01:42:28,000
underconfidence of professional researchers and practitioners in economics, medicine, and whatnot.

36
01:42:28,000 --> 01:42:36,000
You think of episodes like how forever we thought that fat was the problem, and now it's carbs.

37
01:42:36,000 --> 01:42:39,000
And social desirability bias.

38
01:42:39,000 --> 01:42:45,000
So once people say, it's the fat that makes you fat, and like any time you say, what?

39
01:42:45,000 --> 01:42:47,000
The fat makes you fat.

40
01:42:47,000 --> 01:42:53,000
Once that saying becomes socially accepted and standard, then anyone saying something else,

41
01:42:53,000 --> 01:42:56,000
it's almost like you're saying, so you want people to be fat, huh?

42
01:42:56,000 --> 01:42:58,000
You just think it's great for people to die of obesity.

43
01:42:58,000 --> 01:42:59,000
That's the way.

44
01:42:59,000 --> 01:43:01,000
I'm saying that's the wrong theory.

45
01:43:01,000 --> 01:43:04,000
What's the wrong theory?

46
01:43:04,000 --> 01:43:16,000
We're supposed to like, when did you become a paid disciple of big fat?

47
01:43:16,000 --> 01:43:17,000
So real-world significance.

48
01:43:17,000 --> 01:43:24,000
This is something where, over time I have moved the view that this matters more than I initially thought.

49
01:43:24,000 --> 01:43:26,000
I am not constantly moving this direction.

50
01:43:26,000 --> 01:43:29,000
I stabilized a while back.

51
01:43:29,000 --> 01:43:37,000
But some of the experiments show that you've got a lot of problems, but what real-world mistakes can be attributed to them?

52
01:43:37,000 --> 01:43:43,000
So like in the week where we do public choice, I'll mention, I think that this describes political views so well,

53
01:43:43,000 --> 01:43:51,000
and so painfully well, just to see the opinions that people have on subjects that they couldn't possibly know much of anything about.

54
01:43:51,000 --> 01:43:56,000
Just the way that everybody knows that elasticities work the way they want them to work,

55
01:43:56,000 --> 01:44:01,000
and they know it before they even know what elasticity they need for the answer.

56
01:44:01,000 --> 01:44:06,000
So imagine you're teaching someone elasticity, and then before that, say,

57
01:44:06,000 --> 01:44:10,000
so what do you think elasticity of labor demand is, high or low?

58
01:44:10,000 --> 01:44:16,000
And until you have actually got them to think about, well, which answer applies to my view on the minimum wage is true,

59
01:44:16,000 --> 01:44:18,000
people will probably give you one answer.

60
01:44:18,000 --> 01:44:20,000
It's like, well, I don't know.

61
01:44:20,000 --> 01:44:21,000
It doesn't seem like that.

62
01:44:21,000 --> 01:44:23,000
Why would I have an opinion about the elasticity of labor demand?

63
01:44:23,000 --> 01:44:26,000
And then you say, well, if labor demand is highly inelastic,

64
01:44:26,000 --> 01:44:30,000
it means the minimum wage doesn't cause much unemployment, but it's highly elastic when it does.

65
01:44:30,000 --> 01:44:33,000
Well, then, man, it's so inelastic.

66
01:44:33,000 --> 01:44:35,000
It's highly inelastic, man.

67
01:44:35,000 --> 01:44:40,000
And this is one you could actually go and try tricking people and actually just tell them one or the other

68
01:44:40,000 --> 01:44:46,000
and see if they just leap to whatever elasticity assumption gets them the result they want.

69
01:44:46,000 --> 01:44:52,000
I mean, it might be too hard to actually do experimentally because it would involve an elasticity lecture, but still.

70
01:44:52,000 --> 01:44:55,000
It seems like it's interesting.

71
01:44:55,000 --> 01:44:59,000
I guess it would even work if you just said, like, what was the slope of the demand curve?

72
01:44:59,000 --> 01:45:02,000
That would also give the same qualitative result.

73
01:45:02,000 --> 01:45:03,000
Was there a question, Nathan?

74
01:45:03,000 --> 01:45:09,000
Might that be reflective of them, to be charitable to people who are displaying this bias,

75
01:45:09,000 --> 01:45:15,000
of them knowing something about the empirical literature but not understanding the underlying economics?

76
01:45:15,000 --> 01:45:19,000
So if someone thinks, oh, well, I know there's a well-done, hard-cruiter-like study,

77
01:45:19,000 --> 01:45:25,000
I'm going to say that probably the data, the elasticity reflects what is shown in that data.

78
01:45:25,000 --> 01:45:31,000
Yes, that would be perfectly fine if you had some independent reason of thinking that the hard-cruiter study was accurate

79
01:45:31,000 --> 01:45:33,000
other than that it gave you the result you wanted.

80
01:45:33,000 --> 01:45:38,000
Although, in order to do that, you would have to understand elasticity in the first place, so it seems unlikely.

81
01:45:38,000 --> 01:45:40,000
But theoretically, of course, it's possible.

82
01:45:40,000 --> 01:45:45,000
I mean, just imagine that someone knows an incredibly trustworthy person who just reads a lot of things

83
01:45:45,000 --> 01:45:47,000
and just tells them the answers.

84
01:45:47,000 --> 01:45:51,000
Any time that they audit the person, what they say is perfectly accurate, that would be fine.

85
01:45:51,000 --> 01:45:54,000
But how many people actually have a person like that in their lives?

86
01:45:54,000 --> 01:45:56,000
Where are these people?

87
01:45:56,000 --> 01:45:58,000
My roommate. At least he tells me he's always right.

88
01:45:58,000 --> 01:45:59,000
He tells you he's right.

89
01:45:59,000 --> 01:46:04,000
So the auditing is important, where you have to at least once in a while just double-check that what they're saying is right

90
01:46:04,000 --> 01:46:08,000
so that you aren't simply saying, well, look, how accurate are you?

91
01:46:08,000 --> 01:46:09,000
Totally accurate.

92
01:46:09,000 --> 01:46:10,000
Okay, then.

93
01:46:10,000 --> 01:46:11,000
You're like, well, great.

94
01:46:11,000 --> 01:46:13,000
Now let's go and ask you all the questions.

95
01:46:13,000 --> 01:46:18,000
I mean, it answers everything for this one person who's told me it's totally accurate.

96
01:46:18,000 --> 01:46:19,000
All right.

97
01:46:19,000 --> 01:46:25,000
So now something else you can say about belief perseverance is once you have a lot of evidence,

98
01:46:25,000 --> 01:46:28,000
it is reasonable to not update much anymore.

99
01:46:28,000 --> 01:46:32,000
So that can be true, and yet once you've seen 1,000 observations,

100
01:46:32,000 --> 01:46:39,000
it's very reasonable to not noticeably budge when 1,000 first observations comes along.

101
01:46:39,000 --> 01:46:43,000
So I have the following arrogant view about the news.

102
01:46:43,000 --> 01:46:46,000
Look, I have spent 40 years reading history.

103
01:46:46,000 --> 01:46:50,000
I understand the kinds of things that happen in the world,

104
01:46:50,000 --> 01:46:54,000
and the stuff in the news is in no way surprising to me at all, really.

105
