15
01:60:32,360 --> 01:60:34,960
It's a judgment about what people care about,

16
01:60:34,960 --> 01:60:36,520
what's interesting to people.

17
01:60:36,520 --> 01:60:38,200
And what's interesting to people?

18
01:60:38,200 --> 01:60:39,680
Are numbers interesting to people?

19
01:60:39,680 --> 01:60:41,360
No.

20
01:60:41,360 --> 01:60:44,080
Things that are vivid, things that are exciting.

21
01:60:44,080 --> 01:60:46,120
But also, things that are horrifying,

22
01:60:46,120 --> 01:60:48,520
things that are scary, things that interest people

23
01:60:48,520 --> 01:60:51,920
are generally not only not necessarily good things,

24
01:60:51,920 --> 01:60:55,000
they're just things that strongly affect any emotion.

25
01:60:55,000 --> 01:60:57,120
Things that are really disgusting are news.

26
01:60:57,120 --> 01:60:59,480
Things that are really scary are news.

27
01:60:59,480 --> 01:61:02,440
Things that are really happy are news, although not as much so.

28
01:61:02,440 --> 01:61:04,200
Things that are really sad are news.

29
01:61:04,200 --> 01:61:05,000
So let's see.

30
01:61:05,000 --> 01:61:08,280
So the six primary emotions are, what again?

31
01:61:08,280 --> 01:61:09,240
Let's see.

32
01:61:09,240 --> 01:61:11,040
So happiness, surprise.

33
01:61:11,040 --> 01:61:12,400
Things that are really surprising,

34
01:61:12,400 --> 01:61:13,360
that's news, of course.

35
01:61:13,360 --> 01:61:17,600
Happiness, surprise, sadness, anger, disgust.

36
01:61:17,600 --> 01:61:18,600
What's the other one?

37
01:61:18,600 --> 01:61:19,840
I don't know.

38
01:61:19,840 --> 01:61:21,360
That's not a primary emotion.

39
01:61:21,360 --> 01:61:22,320
Surprise.

40
01:61:22,320 --> 01:61:23,840
Yes.

41
01:61:23,840 --> 01:61:25,160
Did I say anger?

42
01:61:25,160 --> 01:61:26,760
Yeah.

43
01:61:26,760 --> 01:61:27,260
Anger.

44
01:61:27,260 --> 01:61:28,720
Let's see.

45
01:61:28,720 --> 01:61:30,200
So anyway, there's another six.

46
01:61:30,200 --> 01:61:30,700
All right.

47
01:61:30,700 --> 01:61:31,280
There's one in the middle.

48
01:61:31,280 --> 01:61:34,040
Anyway, so anything that appeals to those basic emotions

49
01:61:34,040 --> 01:61:36,000
is news, even if it's not reported yet.

50
01:61:36,000 --> 01:61:38,040
So today my Facebook feed filled up

51
01:61:38,040 --> 01:61:41,080
with a guy who was thrown off a plane.

52
01:61:41,080 --> 01:61:42,600
How is that newsworthy?

53
01:61:42,600 --> 01:61:45,760
Oh, but did you see how he was savagely beaten?

54
01:61:45,760 --> 01:61:46,320
All right.

55
01:61:46,320 --> 01:61:48,240
Do you know how many people were murdered today?

56
01:61:48,240 --> 01:61:50,040
Like, what happened to that guy is nothing

57
01:61:50,040 --> 01:61:52,680
compared to what happened to like 1,000 other people

58
01:61:52,680 --> 01:61:54,400
on Earth.

59
01:61:54,400 --> 01:61:57,200
And furthermore, what kind of a person

60
01:61:57,200 --> 01:61:59,520
gets beaten up because they were thrown out of a plane?

61
01:61:59,520 --> 01:62:00,440
How does that happen?

62
01:62:00,440 --> 01:62:04,480
Sounds very unlikely that he was totally blameless here.

63
01:62:04,480 --> 01:62:08,240
Sounds like fact.

64
01:62:08,240 --> 01:62:11,000
If, say, my brother got thrown, like got beaten up,

65
01:62:11,000 --> 01:62:12,880
and he was thrown off a plane, I would totally

66
01:62:12,880 --> 01:62:13,880
side with the airline.

67
01:62:13,880 --> 01:62:20,040
But anyway, unless I knew the person was completely

68
01:62:20,040 --> 01:62:21,600
blameless, that kind of person, I

69
01:62:21,600 --> 01:62:23,100
would always side with the airlines.

70
01:62:23,100 --> 01:62:25,440
Because that person's probably just making a mountain out

71
01:62:25,440 --> 01:62:28,040
of a molehill, and many things escalate.

72
01:62:28,040 --> 01:62:29,480
So why is that news?

73
01:62:29,480 --> 01:62:33,160
One difficult person.

74
01:62:33,160 --> 01:62:34,360
That's me.

75
01:62:34,360 --> 01:62:35,360
All right.

76
01:62:35,360 --> 01:62:37,040
Now, so that's availability heuristic.

77
01:62:37,040 --> 01:62:40,760
Now another common technique for estimating probabilities

78
01:62:40,760 --> 01:62:44,200
is just to compare a particular case to stereotypes,

79
01:62:44,200 --> 01:62:45,800
and then go with a better match.

80
01:62:45,800 --> 01:62:47,800
Which one is a better match?

81
01:62:47,800 --> 01:62:49,240
And this has also a name.

82
01:62:49,240 --> 01:62:51,360
It's called the representative heuristic.

83
01:62:51,360 --> 01:62:54,600
And of course, it goes along with a parallel

84
01:62:54,600 --> 01:62:57,600
representativeness bias.

85
01:62:57,600 --> 01:63:00,480
The representative heuristic, and there's also the bias.

86
01:63:00,480 --> 01:63:02,880
The A, and there's the B.

87
01:63:02,880 --> 01:63:06,920
So here is a classic example.

88
01:63:06,920 --> 01:63:08,880
Someone says, what's more likely?

89
01:63:08,880 --> 01:63:11,840
There's a Chinese professor who teaches Chinese literature,

90
01:63:11,840 --> 01:63:16,240
or a Chinese professor who teaches psychology.

91
01:63:16,240 --> 01:63:18,840
Well, what's our stereotype of Chinese literature professors?

92
01:63:18,840 --> 01:63:20,960
What's their ethnicity?

93
01:63:20,960 --> 01:63:21,840
Chinese.

94
01:63:21,840 --> 01:63:24,360
What's our stereotype of psychology professors?

95
01:63:24,360 --> 01:63:25,200
Their ethnicity?

96
01:63:25,200 --> 01:63:26,080
Austrian.

97
01:63:26,080 --> 01:63:27,000
Austrian, white, yeah.

98
01:63:27,000 --> 01:63:28,720
So Jews, something like that.

99
01:63:28,720 --> 01:63:30,560
Anyway, it's definitely not Chinese.

100
01:63:30,560 --> 01:63:31,200
All right.

101
01:63:31,200 --> 01:63:34,520
So and it's very tenuously.

102
01:63:34,520 --> 01:63:37,080
So one fits more than the other.

103
01:63:37,080 --> 01:63:37,880
All right.

104
01:63:37,880 --> 01:63:40,600
So now what's wrong with this?

105
01:63:40,600 --> 01:63:42,640
Well, sometimes nothing.

106
01:63:42,640 --> 01:63:44,560
But there is one basic problem here,

107
01:63:44,560 --> 01:63:48,440
and that is a tendency to ignore base rates.

108
01:63:48,440 --> 01:63:50,080
So when you're deciding, so what's

109
01:63:50,080 --> 01:63:52,160
more likely that that Chinese professor is

110
01:63:52,160 --> 01:63:54,720
a professor of Chinese literature or psychology,

111
01:63:54,720 --> 01:63:56,400
there's a tendency to ignore the fact

112
01:63:56,400 --> 01:63:58,960
that there are hardly any Chinese literature

113
01:63:58,960 --> 01:64:01,000
professors in the United States.

114
01:64:01,000 --> 01:64:03,920
And there's a vast number of psychologists.

115
01:64:03,920 --> 01:64:06,840
So even if 100% of the Chinese literature professors

116
01:64:06,840 --> 01:64:09,600
were Chinese, it's still quite likely

117
01:64:09,600 --> 01:64:13,280
that there are more Chinese psychology professors,

118
01:64:13,280 --> 01:64:15,880
because there's a ton of psychology professors.

119
01:64:15,880 --> 01:64:18,220
So if there's 100 Chinese literature professors in the US

120
01:64:18,220 --> 01:64:23,000
and they're all Chinese, and there are 10,000 psychology

121
01:64:23,000 --> 01:64:25,840
professors and 5% of them are Chinese,

122
01:64:25,840 --> 01:64:28,040
there are still far more Chinese psychologists

123
01:64:28,040 --> 01:64:32,080
than there are Chinese literature professors.

124
01:64:32,080 --> 01:64:33,640
And that's the problem.

125
01:64:33,640 --> 01:64:34,720
All right.

126
01:64:34,720 --> 01:64:36,400
So and that's where the bias comes

127
01:64:36,400 --> 01:64:39,360
from, from ignoring base rates and not

128
01:64:39,360 --> 01:64:42,520
doing a proper adjustment for the general frequency.

129
01:64:42,520 --> 01:64:44,360
Now notice that problem I've just given you

130
01:64:44,360 --> 01:64:49,760
about the false positives, that fits in this bowl very well.

131
01:64:49,760 --> 01:64:52,120
Because what is our stereotype about,

132
01:64:52,120 --> 01:64:54,880
so the test is perfectly accurate.

133
01:64:54,880 --> 01:64:57,600
So we'll put a test, if you have the disease,

134
01:64:57,600 --> 01:64:59,080
then you always test positive.

135
01:64:59,080 --> 01:65:02,640
And 95% of people who don't have the disease test negative.

136
01:65:02,640 --> 01:65:05,600
So then our stereotype says, if you test it positive,

137
01:65:05,600 --> 01:65:09,520
then you got, so sick people test positive.

138
01:65:09,520 --> 01:65:11,360
And well, people test negative.

139
01:65:11,360 --> 01:65:12,680
Those are two true stereotypes.

140
01:65:12,680 --> 01:65:14,040
They're both correct.

141
01:65:14,040 --> 01:65:16,600
100% of people who test positive have the disease.

142
01:65:16,600 --> 01:65:19,880
95% of people who test negative don't have the disease.

143
01:65:19,880 --> 01:65:21,640
So then where are we going wrong?

144
01:65:21,640 --> 01:65:24,400
We're going wrong because we're getting that only 1% of people

145
01:65:24,400 --> 01:65:27,040
have the disease.

146
01:65:27,040 --> 01:65:29,960
So that is a base rate.

147
01:65:29,960 --> 01:65:32,760
And when you factor in that base rate in the calculation,

148
01:65:32,760 --> 01:65:36,120
then you realize, my god, most of the people who test positive

149
01:65:36,120 --> 01:65:38,400
are still perfectly fine.

150
01:65:38,400 --> 01:65:41,360
They're still perfectly healthy because it's so rare

151
01:65:41,360 --> 01:65:46,520
that someone actually has this disease.

152
01:65:46,520 --> 01:65:47,000
OK.

153
01:65:47,000 --> 01:65:50,400
So now a class experiment involving

154
01:65:50,400 --> 01:65:55,120
a representative misbias involves a strange conference.

155
01:65:55,120 --> 01:65:57,840
This is a conference where engineers and psychologists

156
01:65:57,840 --> 01:66:01,320
get together to discuss their shared issues,

157
01:66:01,320 --> 01:66:02,880
whatever those might be.

158
01:66:02,880 --> 01:66:04,240
I don't know why they'd have one.

159
01:66:04,240 --> 01:66:06,160
But anyway, so the standard manipulation

160
01:66:06,160 --> 01:66:10,400
is some people are told that 70% of the attendees are engineers.

161
01:66:10,400 --> 01:66:14,600
Others are told that only 30% of the attendees are engineers.

162
01:66:14,600 --> 01:66:16,720
And then they say, all right, so you

163
01:66:16,720 --> 01:66:19,000
meet someone who has horn-rimmed glasses and a pocket

164
01:66:19,000 --> 01:66:20,680
protector.

165
01:66:20,680 --> 01:66:23,880
Now, if you don't know any engineers or that, anyway,

166
01:66:23,880 --> 01:66:25,360
so my dad was an engineer.

167
01:66:25,360 --> 01:66:27,680
And in the 1980s, he definitely had horn-rimmed glasses

168
01:66:27,680 --> 01:66:29,320
and a pocket protector.

169
01:66:29,320 --> 01:66:31,840
So he totally fit the stereotype.

170
01:66:31,840 --> 01:66:35,600
But the strange thing in the experiment

171
01:66:35,600 --> 01:66:39,560
is that you can go and tell people 70-30 or 30-70

172
01:66:39,560 --> 01:66:41,760
and still their conditional probability that someone

173
01:66:41,760 --> 01:66:44,120
is an engineer, given that they have horn-rimmed glasses

174
01:66:44,120 --> 01:66:46,400
and a pocket protector, doesn't change.

175
01:66:46,400 --> 01:66:49,240
And it should change.

176
01:66:49,240 --> 01:66:50,960
The only way in which it shouldn't change

177
01:66:50,960 --> 01:66:53,080
is if you really believe that 100% of all engineers

178
01:66:53,080 --> 01:66:55,280
have horn-rimmed glasses and a pocket protector

179
01:66:55,280 --> 01:66:57,240
and 0% of psychologists have that.

180
01:66:57,240 --> 01:67:00,080
Then you could just say 100% regardless of the base rates,

181
01:67:00,080 --> 01:67:02,040
and it wouldn't matter.

182
01:67:02,040 --> 01:67:05,120
Just like in that false positive experiment,

183
01:67:05,120 --> 01:67:08,640
if there's a 0% false positive rate and a 0% false negative

184
01:67:08,640 --> 01:67:10,680
rate, then of course 100% of people

185
01:67:10,680 --> 01:67:14,160
who test positive have the disease.

186
01:67:14,160 --> 01:67:17,600
But people realize that it's not really people

187
01:67:17,600 --> 01:67:20,000
are smart enough to know, well, sure, there's

188
01:67:20,000 --> 01:67:23,080
going to be some psychologists who have a pocket protector

189
01:67:23,080 --> 01:67:25,360
and some engineers that don't have the pocket protector.

190
01:67:25,360 --> 01:67:27,200
It's not like that.

191
01:67:27,200 --> 01:67:30,280
It's too cartoonish to think that every engineer dresses

192
01:67:30,280 --> 01:67:32,600
one way, every psychologist dresses the other way.

193
01:67:32,600 --> 01:67:38,320
But they fail to do the logical inference of saying that we

194
01:67:38,320 --> 01:67:40,640
should then adjust for the fact that in one scenario,

195
01:67:40,640 --> 01:67:42,400
there's more engineers than psychologists.

196
01:67:42,400 --> 01:67:45,560
In another one, there's less engineers than psychologists.

197
01:67:45,560 --> 01:67:48,360
That's a problem.

198
01:67:48,360 --> 01:67:50,560
And then another one of my favorite examples here

199
01:67:50,560 --> 01:67:53,520
is gaydar.

200
01:67:53,520 --> 01:67:55,760
So a man is wearing a pink shirt.

201
01:67:55,760 --> 01:67:58,400
Probability that he's gay.

202
01:67:58,400 --> 01:68:00,960
Well, there is a stereotype that gay men are perfectly fine

203
01:68:00,960 --> 01:68:02,280
wearing pink shirts.

204
01:68:02,280 --> 01:68:04,800
And there's also a stereotype, possibly

205
01:68:04,800 --> 01:68:07,280
caused by the first stereotype, that straight men

206
01:68:07,280 --> 01:68:08,600
don't wear pink shirts.

207
01:68:08,600 --> 01:68:10,840
But again, it's not 100%.

208
01:68:10,840 --> 01:68:14,280
And so there is a tendency to just say

209
01:68:14,280 --> 01:68:16,560
you see a man in a pink shirt and say, that guy's gay.

210
01:68:16,560 --> 01:68:17,920
My gaydar's going off.

211
01:68:17,920 --> 01:68:20,320
But if you go and look at sex research,

212
01:68:20,320 --> 01:68:23,760
it says, look, only 2% of men are gay.

213
01:68:23,760 --> 01:68:28,040
That's got to go and say, look, if only 2% are gay,

214
01:68:28,040 --> 01:68:31,680
then there's probably a lot more straight men

215
01:68:31,680 --> 01:68:33,760
wearing pink shirts, even if a much smaller fraction

216
01:68:33,760 --> 01:68:36,600
of straight men would wear pink shirts.

217
01:68:36,600 --> 01:68:38,480
See, the funniest example of this

218
01:68:38,480 --> 01:68:42,280
is when I was at Princeton, there

219
01:68:42,280 --> 01:68:45,680
was an attempt to start a holiday called Gay Jeans Day.

220
01:68:45,680 --> 01:68:47,880
And it's one where if you're gay,

221
01:68:47,880 --> 01:68:50,160
you're supposed to wear jeans that day.

222
01:68:50,160 --> 01:68:52,360
And you're like, what?

223
01:68:52,360 --> 01:68:56,120
Well, it's a very clever and thoughtful holiday.

224
01:68:56,120 --> 01:68:57,880
Because the whole point of the holiday,

225
01:68:57,880 --> 01:69:00,040
the real point of the holiday, is

226
01:69:00,040 --> 01:69:02,120
to see whether straight men will deliberately not

227
01:69:02,120 --> 01:69:03,840
wear jeans on the day out of fear

228
01:69:03,840 --> 01:69:06,680
that they will be misidentified as gay.

229
01:69:06,680 --> 01:69:09,200
And it does then make people confront, well,

230
01:69:09,200 --> 01:69:10,320
I'm not homophobic at all.

231
01:69:10,320 --> 01:69:12,120
But if someone, a total stranger,

232
01:69:12,120 --> 01:69:14,400
thought I was gay, that would be the worst thing ever.

233
01:69:14,400 --> 01:69:17,040
So I better not wear jeans today.

234
01:69:17,040 --> 01:69:18,680
It's an interesting idea.

235
01:69:18,680 --> 01:69:23,400
And it is building on this representative heuristic

236
01:69:23,400 --> 01:69:26,040
of saying, well, once people know about the holiday,

237
01:69:26,040 --> 01:69:29,360
then the stereotype will be gays, jeans equals gay,

238
01:69:29,360 --> 01:69:30,560
no jeans equals not gay.

239
01:69:30,560 --> 01:69:32,680
And then, well, you've got to make sure

240
01:69:32,680 --> 01:69:35,840
that total strangers don't make a mistake about my orientation.

241
01:69:35,840 --> 01:69:39,920
Because Lord knows where that could lead.

242
01:69:39,920 --> 01:69:41,400
So questions?

243
01:69:41,400 --> 01:69:43,120
Questions about this?

244
01:69:43,120 --> 01:69:44,240
All right.

245
01:69:44,240 --> 01:69:46,520
So let's see.

246
01:69:46,520 --> 01:69:47,640
Oh, yeah.

247
01:69:47,640 --> 01:69:50,920
Another fun example of this is if someone

248
01:69:50,920 --> 01:69:54,160
claims to have an IQ that's better than one person out

249
01:69:54,160 --> 01:69:57,320
of a billion, well, one possibility

250
01:69:57,320 --> 01:69:59,440
is they really are one out of a billion.

251
01:69:59,440 --> 01:70:02,840
But how many people are actually one out of a billion?

252
01:70:02,840 --> 01:70:03,880
Very few.

253
01:70:03,880 --> 01:70:06,480
And what would be the false positive rate for identifying

254
01:70:06,480 --> 01:70:08,520
people that smart?

255
01:70:08,520 --> 01:70:10,080
Probably enormous.

256
01:70:10,080 --> 01:70:13,160
So if you figure there's only the seven smartest people

257
01:70:13,160 --> 01:70:14,640
on Earth, there's seven of them.

258
01:70:14,640 --> 01:70:16,760
But how many people would get falsely identified

259
01:70:16,760 --> 01:70:19,600
as being one of the seven smartest people on Earth?

260
01:70:19,600 --> 01:70:22,000
There could be a million people if you're so identified.

261
01:70:22,000 --> 01:70:23,160
And so the conditional probability

262
01:70:23,160 --> 01:70:24,920
that you are that smart, given that you've

263
01:70:24,920 --> 01:70:28,920
been identified as such by some test or other, is super low.

264
01:70:28,920 --> 01:70:31,000
There actually is a society called JICA Society

265
01:70:31,000 --> 01:70:35,720
where they claim to be a one in a billion level of intellect.

266
01:70:35,720 --> 01:70:38,240
And at least my last check, there were three members.

267
01:70:38,240 --> 01:70:40,440
They claim they're still looking for the other four.

268
01:70:40,440 --> 01:70:41,520
We haven't found them yet.

269
01:70:41,520 --> 01:70:44,120
But if you think you can qualify, let us know.

270
01:70:44,120 --> 01:70:46,560
We'll give a test that only we're smart enough

271
01:70:46,560 --> 01:70:48,160
to actually grade.

272
01:70:48,160 --> 01:70:50,960
Even no one but us can do it.

273
01:70:50,960 --> 01:70:51,960
All right.

274
01:70:51,960 --> 01:70:55,680
So questions about that?

275
01:70:55,680 --> 01:70:59,800
So then let us move on to risk misperceptions.

276
01:70:59,800 --> 01:71:01,520
So remember, the rational explanation

277
01:71:01,520 --> 01:71:05,000
of this assumption says that on average, people's beliefs

278
01:71:05,000 --> 01:71:06,880
are true.

279
01:71:06,880 --> 01:71:09,920
On average, people's beliefs are true.

280
01:71:09,920 --> 01:71:11,400
The average belief is correct.

281
01:71:11,400 --> 01:71:14,000
And that would hold, of course, for assessments of risk,

282
01:71:14,000 --> 01:71:16,080
such as the risk of being killed by a terrorist

283
01:71:16,080 --> 01:71:19,400
or dying in a plane crash or a car crash

284
01:71:19,400 --> 01:71:24,600
or dying from drinking too much water or from alcohol poisoning

285
01:71:24,600 --> 01:71:26,920
or from a brick falling on your head.

286
01:71:26,920 --> 01:71:30,280
All these different risks you face.

287
01:71:30,280 --> 01:71:32,320
So let's see.

288
01:71:32,320 --> 01:71:35,240
Lately, I've taken to asking professors in different areas,

289
01:71:35,240 --> 01:71:37,640
how afraid are you on a scale from 0 to 10

290
01:71:37,640 --> 01:71:39,720
that you're going to say something which will lead

291
01:71:39,720 --> 01:71:42,800
your students to protest you?

292
01:71:42,800 --> 01:71:44,840
And I've been getting some numbers

293
01:71:44,840 --> 01:71:46,560
that seem extraordinarily high, even

294
01:71:46,560 --> 01:71:51,040
for people who don't seem very controversial at all to me.

295
01:71:51,040 --> 01:71:53,440
Whereas I say many controversial things.

296
01:71:53,440 --> 01:71:58,440
And I say, like, 0 to 10, like a 1 and 1 half.

297
01:71:58,440 --> 01:72:02,400
So I think more likely than the average person,

298
01:72:02,400 --> 01:72:04,800
or I think it's astronomically unlikely anyone would ever

299
01:72:04,800 --> 01:72:06,280
protest most professors.

300
01:72:06,280 --> 01:72:09,520
But then, look, the stuff you see in the news,

301
01:72:09,520 --> 01:72:12,040
these are the worst things that happened on Earth that day

302
01:72:12,040 --> 01:72:14,240
in the field of students getting angry over a professor

303
01:72:14,240 --> 01:72:17,360
for making some level of comment.

304
01:72:17,360 --> 01:72:20,880
Because what do you need to have a great story about a professor

305
01:72:20,880 --> 01:72:22,600
being protested?

306
01:72:22,600 --> 01:72:25,760
You want to have the statement be totally inoffensive

307
01:72:25,760 --> 01:72:27,800
and the students to be hysterical.

308
01:72:27,800 --> 01:72:30,000
That's the ideal story.

309
01:72:30,000 --> 01:72:35,200
So professor says that Europe is further north than Africa.

310
01:72:35,200 --> 01:72:38,160
And then the students flip out at him.

311
01:72:38,160 --> 01:72:41,160
All right, that's a perfect story.

312
01:72:41,160 --> 01:72:43,920
Then as the statement becomes crazier,

313
01:72:43,920 --> 01:72:45,460
or the students become more moderate,

314
01:72:45,460 --> 01:72:46,360
the story gets worse.

315
01:72:46,360 --> 01:72:48,200
So the best stories are these ones

316
01:72:48,200 --> 01:72:50,520
where students are upset for no reason.

317
01:72:50,520 --> 01:72:53,360
But there's a vast number of professors in the world.

318
01:72:53,360 --> 01:72:55,640
And there are journalists trying to find these stories

319
01:72:55,640 --> 01:72:58,520
and share them and make you very aware of them.

320
01:72:58,520 --> 01:73:00,680
So I still say that my risk is really low.

321
01:73:00,680 --> 01:73:05,000
I don't want this to be a self-reversing prophecy.

322
01:73:05,000 --> 01:73:07,720
I have never gotten a single student evaluation

323
01:73:07,720 --> 01:73:12,960
that said that I said something offensive, ever, 20 years.

324
01:73:12,960 --> 01:73:15,680
That's what I've gotten.

325
01:73:15,680 --> 01:73:18,600
So what are the odds that I would get something way worse?

326
01:73:18,600 --> 01:73:22,160
So I figured, on one student going and complaining

327
01:73:22,160 --> 01:73:26,240
that you're offensive, that must be way more common than a riot.

328
01:73:26,240 --> 01:73:28,400
I think that one angry student just saying,

329
01:73:28,400 --> 01:73:30,920
yeah, really offended me when he said that thing.

330
01:73:30,920 --> 01:73:32,800
That's got to be way more common than there's

331
01:73:32,800 --> 01:73:36,440
just a giant crowd of protesters swarm me and throw stuff at me.

332
01:73:36,440 --> 01:73:39,520
So that seems like it'd be 1,000 times more common for there

333
01:73:39,520 --> 01:73:40,520
to be one angry student.

334
01:73:40,520 --> 01:73:42,520
And since I haven't even had that problem,

335
01:73:42,520 --> 01:73:44,480
I'm really not scared about the other problem.

336
01:73:44,480 --> 01:73:46,960
All right.

337
01:73:46,960 --> 01:73:51,360
Anyways, but the question is, are people on average

338
01:73:51,360 --> 01:73:52,200
accurate about this?

339
01:73:52,200 --> 01:73:58,480
Maybe I'm the one that is living in a dream about how safe I am.

340
01:73:58,480 --> 01:74:02,880
And actually, I'm just about to go and cause a riot right now.

341
01:74:02,880 --> 01:74:06,720
I am in five, four, three, two, one.

342
01:74:06,720 --> 01:74:08,720
No riot.

343
01:74:08,720 --> 01:74:10,240
And then he said that there wouldn't

344
01:74:10,240 --> 01:74:12,160
be a riot in five seconds.

345
01:74:12,160 --> 01:74:16,560
And that really upset me and caused a riot.

346
01:74:16,560 --> 01:74:18,920
So anyway, there's a big empirical literature

347
01:74:18,920 --> 01:74:21,400
looking at people's assessments of risk.

348
01:74:21,400 --> 01:74:25,080
And again, it's very similar to this calibration thing,

349
01:74:25,080 --> 01:74:27,400
where we're trying to go on this axis.

350
01:74:27,400 --> 01:74:29,440
We put people's beliefs about probability

351
01:74:29,440 --> 01:74:31,040
that something bad is going to happen.

352
01:74:31,040 --> 01:74:33,240
And then here we have the actual probability, which

353
01:74:33,240 --> 01:74:35,200
usually happens to the actual frequency.

354
01:74:35,200 --> 01:74:36,960
So this is subjective belief.

355
01:74:36,960 --> 01:74:38,640
This is an observed frequency.

356
01:74:38,640 --> 01:74:41,000
And then rational expectations means

357
01:74:41,000 --> 01:74:42,920
we're on the 45 degree line.

358
01:74:42,920 --> 01:74:45,800
People think there's an 80% risk of things that really

359
01:74:45,800 --> 01:74:47,360
happen 80% of the time.

360
01:74:47,360 --> 01:74:49,280
They think there's an 8% risk of things that

361
01:74:49,280 --> 01:74:51,360
happen 80% of the time, and so on.

362
01:74:54,440 --> 01:74:56,080
So anyway, empirical literature says

363
01:74:56,080 --> 01:74:59,040
that we're not on the 45 degree line in lots of ways.

364
01:75:01,560 --> 01:75:05,120
So lots of findings and lots of details.

365
01:75:05,120 --> 01:75:08,320
So I'll just tell you the big findings.

366
01:75:08,320 --> 01:75:11,040
Big finding is that we are really bad at handling

367
01:75:11,040 --> 01:75:13,400
low probability events.

368
01:75:13,400 --> 01:75:17,360
So what this says is that if we go and zoom in

369
01:75:17,360 --> 01:75:23,360
on the part of this graph from 0% to 10%,

370
01:75:23,360 --> 01:75:27,360
so we go and zoom in on the really small risks,

371
01:75:27,360 --> 01:75:30,120
that's where we screw up very badly.

372
01:75:30,120 --> 01:75:32,520
And how are we supposed to screw up?

373
01:75:32,520 --> 01:75:35,240
Well, sometimes we go and treat low probability events

374
01:75:35,240 --> 01:75:37,280
as if they have a 0 probability.

375
01:75:37,280 --> 01:75:41,960
So rounding down to 0 is one common thing people do.

376
01:75:41,960 --> 01:75:44,240
Another common thing, though, is rounding up

377
01:75:44,240 --> 01:75:47,760
from 1 in a million to 1 in 100.

378
01:75:47,760 --> 01:75:51,320
So there seems to be this binary response of either saying

379
01:75:51,320 --> 01:75:53,760
it is 0 when it's not.

380
01:75:53,760 --> 01:75:55,680
So a 1 in a million chance of dying

381
01:75:55,680 --> 01:75:57,560
isn't a 0 chance of dying.

382
01:75:57,560 --> 01:75:59,840
If there's a 1 in a million chance of dying daily,

383
01:75:59,840 --> 01:76:03,240
then every day 300 people die in the United States,

384
01:76:03,240 --> 01:76:05,080
which is noticeable, and it adds up.

385
01:76:05,080 --> 01:76:09,040
From 0, of course, then it would be 0 every day.

386
01:76:09,040 --> 01:76:11,840
But then the other problem is treating low probability event

387
01:76:11,840 --> 01:76:14,040
as being much more likely than it really is.

388
01:76:14,040 --> 01:76:16,440
Normally, when people do this, they still

389
01:76:16,440 --> 01:76:20,640
think that it's absolutely low, like maybe it's a 1% chance.

390
01:76:20,640 --> 01:76:22,600
But if it is like a 1 in a billion event

391
01:76:22,600 --> 01:76:24,600
and you're rounding up to 1 in 100,

392
01:76:24,600 --> 01:76:27,720
that leads to massive waste of resources.

393
01:76:27,720 --> 01:76:31,160
And this is probably where terrorism falls.

394
01:76:31,160 --> 01:76:33,000
So I'm not sure if there's actually

395
01:76:33,000 --> 01:76:34,520
any study just asking people, what

396
01:76:34,520 --> 01:76:37,400
do you think your odds are of dying from terrorism?

397
01:76:37,400 --> 01:76:39,880
But what I can tell you is, in terms

398
01:76:39,880 --> 01:76:42,880
of the objective frequency, it is really low.

399
01:76:42,880 --> 01:76:45,920
So it would be like the opposite.

400
01:76:45,920 --> 01:76:49,960
Remember that everybody dies of something eventually, so far.

401
01:76:49,960 --> 01:76:52,120
So an actual risk that it will be terrorism

402
01:76:52,120 --> 01:76:54,160
is under 1 in 10,000.

403
01:76:54,160 --> 01:76:56,480
So if you were to actually go and ask people,

404
01:76:56,480 --> 01:76:59,520
what are the odds that you will ever, in your entire life,

405
01:76:59,520 --> 01:77:02,240
that your cause of death will be terrorism?

406
01:77:02,240 --> 01:77:05,440
I don't know, so what it would be, I mean,

407
01:77:05,440 --> 01:77:07,240
on this, I don't think it's ever been done.

408
01:77:07,240 --> 01:77:09,360
But I think it's got to be at least 1 in 1,000,

409
01:77:09,360 --> 01:77:10,920
maybe even higher than that.

410
01:77:10,920 --> 01:77:13,280
And I would also bet that right after a terrorist attack,

411
01:77:13,280 --> 01:77:15,660
it jumps up, especially in the country that it's happened.

412
01:77:20,520 --> 01:77:23,880
Now, a fun thing about this empirical work

413
01:77:23,880 --> 01:77:26,400
is it's very standard for advocates

414
01:77:26,400 --> 01:77:28,640
of paternalistic safety regulation

415
01:77:28,640 --> 01:77:31,320
to claim that the empirical evidence is totally

416
01:77:31,320 --> 01:77:33,800
on their side.

417
01:77:33,800 --> 01:77:35,280
And again, so what is the idea?

418
01:77:35,280 --> 01:77:41,640
So again, like in a standard model of, say, labor markets,

419
01:77:41,640 --> 01:77:47,160
so suppose that we're talking about nuclear workers, where

420
01:77:47,160 --> 01:77:49,280
there's some probability that a nuclear worker gets

421
01:77:49,280 --> 01:77:51,720
killed in a meltdown.

422
01:77:51,720 --> 01:77:54,880
So if that is the number of hours that are sold,

423
01:77:54,880 --> 01:77:56,040
that's the wage.

424
01:77:56,040 --> 01:77:59,800
This is supply and demand for a totally normal job

425
01:77:59,800 --> 01:78:03,160
without the risk of a nuclear meltdown.

426
01:78:03,160 --> 01:78:05,480
What is our standard supply and demand story

427
01:78:05,480 --> 01:78:07,520
about how the market will be different if there

428
01:78:07,520 --> 01:78:09,040
is a risk of radiation?

429
01:78:09,040 --> 01:78:11,440
You want to do that by radiation?

430
01:78:11,440 --> 01:78:12,840
Supply is going to go to the left.

431
01:78:12,840 --> 01:78:14,600
Yes, supply is lower.

432
01:78:14,600 --> 01:78:16,320
Supply is lower.

433
01:78:16,320 --> 01:78:17,840
And as a result of this, of course,

434
01:78:17,840 --> 01:78:19,560
what happens to the wage?

435
01:78:19,560 --> 01:78:20,400
Goes up.

436
01:78:20,400 --> 01:78:22,760
And this gives employers an incentive to do what?

437
01:78:25,520 --> 01:78:28,000
To try to mitigate the risk.

438
01:78:28,000 --> 01:78:30,720
It gives employers an incentive to go and do it.

439
01:78:30,720 --> 01:78:34,160
And if they can go and get rid of the risk for a cost

440
01:78:34,160 --> 01:78:36,080
less than the end of the wage premium,

441
01:78:36,080 --> 01:78:38,440
they will get rid of the risk entirely.

442
01:78:38,440 --> 01:78:40,960
If they can get rid of half the risk

443
01:78:40,960 --> 01:78:43,960
and get rid of, say, 2 3rds of the wage premium,

444
01:78:43,960 --> 01:78:46,400
but then the remaining half the risk costs more than that,

445
01:78:46,400 --> 01:78:47,480
then they'll get rid of half the risk.

446
01:78:47,480 --> 01:78:49,940
And then you'll just live with the remaining half the risk.

447
01:78:49,940 --> 01:78:53,920
And this is, of course, all exactly what social optimality

448
01:78:53,920 --> 01:78:56,000
will tell you to do.

449
01:78:56,000 --> 01:78:59,360
So normally, when people who know economics

450
01:78:59,360 --> 01:79:01,520
want to argue for risk regulation,

451
01:79:01,520 --> 01:79:03,880
they'll say, well, that's what the model is.

452
01:79:03,880 --> 01:79:07,680
But you have to remember that people are rational about risk.

453
01:79:07,680 --> 01:79:10,680
Now, there's just one problem.

454
01:79:10,680 --> 01:79:13,880
If that is your story, then you need

455
01:79:13,880 --> 01:79:16,200
to actually go and look at the detailed empirics

456
01:79:16,200 --> 01:79:19,160
to figure out whether those particular regulations

457
01:79:19,160 --> 01:79:23,440
that you were pushing for do, in fact, compensate for the risk

458
01:79:23,440 --> 01:79:26,400
misperceptions, because here's the thing

459
01:79:26,400 --> 01:79:27,400
about safety regulation.

460
01:79:27,400 --> 01:79:29,280
How many safety regulations are concerned

461
01:79:29,280 --> 01:79:30,760
with making people get more safety

462
01:79:30,760 --> 01:79:33,120
than they would get on their own?

463
01:79:33,120 --> 01:79:34,480
Probably not a lot.

464
01:79:34,480 --> 01:79:34,980
No.

465
01:79:34,980 --> 01:79:39,600
Regulations that try to make you have people get more safety.

466
01:79:39,600 --> 01:79:40,100
Yeah.

467
01:79:40,100 --> 01:79:43,760
Almost all risk regulation is about pushing more safety.

468
01:79:43,760 --> 01:79:45,160
What fraction of safety regulation

469
01:79:45,160 --> 01:79:46,640
is about trying to get people to not

470
01:79:46,640 --> 01:79:49,440
take unnecessary precautions that are not numerically

471
01:79:49,440 --> 01:79:51,040
justified?

472
01:79:51,040 --> 01:79:51,800
Virtually none.

473
01:79:51,800 --> 01:79:55,520
I can think of a very small number of examples.

474
01:79:55,520 --> 01:79:58,160
Now, this is odd based upon what I just

475
01:79:58,160 --> 01:80:23,120
told you about the evidence.


1
01:80:00,000 --> 01:80:03,280
Because if people sometimes round down to zero,

2
01:80:03,280 --> 01:80:06,080
that is a reason to go and say you have to go

3
01:80:06,080 --> 01:80:08,280
and adopt these additional safety precautions

4
01:80:08,660 --> 01:80:11,160
because you think that nothing can hurt you,

5
01:80:11,160 --> 01:80:11,760
but it really can't.

6
01:80:12,660 --> 01:80:14,700
But there should also be a bunch of regulations

7
01:80:14,700 --> 01:80:17,680
on the other side saying that you're not allowed

8
01:80:17,680 --> 01:80:19,000
to offer certain precautions

9
01:80:19,000 --> 01:80:20,900
because people are exaggerating a danger.

10
01:80:22,320 --> 01:80:23,660
And again, there's almost none of those.

11
01:80:24,020 --> 01:80:26,720
The best example I know of, like I can think of two actually.

12
01:80:26,880 --> 01:80:28,460
Well, one's real and one's proposed.

13
01:80:28,460 --> 01:80:31,640
So there is a regulation in the book saying that it's illegal

14
01:80:31,640 --> 01:80:33,400
for airlines to advertise their safety records.

15
01:80:35,400 --> 01:80:37,800
And in terms of the human tendency

16
01:80:37,800 --> 01:80:39,840
to exaggerate the risk of low probability events,

17
01:80:39,840 --> 01:80:40,860
this makes some sense, right?

18
01:80:41,440 --> 01:80:44,320
Because if airlines were to go and be advertising their safety

19
01:80:44,320 --> 01:80:45,460
regulations all the time,

20
01:80:45,840 --> 01:80:48,700
this would further amplify human's tendency

21
01:80:48,700 --> 01:80:49,660
to be terrified of flying.

22
01:80:50,240 --> 01:80:51,220
And then we'd kill babies.

23
01:80:51,980 --> 01:80:52,200
All right?

24
01:80:52,200 --> 01:80:53,360
We don't want to kill babies, right?

25
01:80:53,740 --> 01:80:54,420
The babies should live.

26
01:80:56,140 --> 01:80:56,460
All right?

27
01:80:56,460 --> 01:80:59,860
So that's one where that is a genuine one where it seems,

28
01:81:00,220 --> 01:81:02,340
again, probably the actual reason is just rent seeking

29
01:81:02,340 --> 01:81:04,500
and the airlines don't want anyone talking about it.

30
01:81:04,940 --> 01:81:07,100
But it does actually serve a useful function

31
01:81:07,420 --> 01:81:10,280
of getting people to worry a bit less about something

32
01:81:10,280 --> 01:81:11,980
that they really shouldn't worry about at all.

33
01:81:13,620 --> 01:81:18,360
Another example of this is Ralph Nader advocates banning all

34
01:81:18,360 --> 01:81:20,300
sunblock with an SPF over 15.

35
01:81:22,080 --> 01:81:23,420
Because he claims the science says

36
01:81:23,420 --> 01:81:26,960
that nobody needs more than 15.

37
01:81:26,960 --> 01:81:27,060
All right?

38
01:81:27,060 --> 01:81:29,280
And then it's just companies that are taking advantage

39
01:81:29,280 --> 01:81:31,040
of the ignorance of people who don't realize

40
01:81:31,040 --> 01:81:32,760
that 15 is full protection.

41
01:81:33,220 --> 01:81:34,280
And they're just ripping you off.

42
01:81:34,280 --> 01:81:36,000
And so the law should go and stop this.

43
01:81:37,040 --> 01:81:37,280
All right?

44
01:81:37,280 --> 01:81:40,180
Now, as a person who has tried 15 as well as higher than 15,

45
01:81:40,820 --> 01:81:42,140
I just think Ralph Nader is wrong.

46
01:81:43,640 --> 01:81:45,620
Right? So that's my natural skin tone.

47
01:81:46,060 --> 01:81:49,980
And therefore, like I have gotten badly burned with 15,

48
01:81:49,980 --> 01:81:52,520
especially remember that you may be using it

49
01:81:52,520 --> 01:81:54,300
when you're swimming or at the beach.

50
01:81:54,640 --> 01:81:57,580
Where even if 15 is perfectly fine, if you don't do anything

51
01:81:57,580 --> 01:82:01,100
that dilutes it, you may still want to have the SPF 90.

52
01:82:01,920 --> 01:82:05,700
So that if it loses 90% of its efficacy, you're still protected.

53
01:82:07,260 --> 01:82:09,380
So anyway, but anyway, if Ralph Nader were right,

54
01:82:09,380 --> 01:82:11,180
that would actually be a regulation trying

55
01:82:11,180 --> 01:82:12,740
to discourage excessive precaution.

56
01:82:13,320 --> 01:82:14,440
But those are super rare.

57
01:82:14,660 --> 01:82:17,000
And instead, virtually all regulation is just trying

58
01:82:17,000 --> 01:82:18,900
to make people even more paranoid than they already are.

59
01:82:18,900 --> 01:82:22,940
Sometimes, you know, sometimes of course there are risks

60
01:82:22,940 --> 01:82:24,020
that people are rounding down on.

61
01:82:24,020 --> 01:82:25,480
But there's other risks that people are really rounding

62
01:82:25,480 --> 01:82:25,900
up on.

63
01:82:26,360 --> 01:82:27,380
And again, terrorism.

64
01:82:28,080 --> 01:82:29,860
Over a trillion dollars spent by government trying

65
01:82:29,860 --> 01:82:31,640
to fight a nearly nonexistent risk.

66
01:82:33,040 --> 01:82:35,220
Right? And it's like how many government programs are here

67
01:82:35,220 --> 01:82:38,520
to say, look, let's be numerous about terrorism, people.

68
01:82:39,040 --> 01:82:42,900
How many school curricula just have a section on probability

69
01:82:43,080 --> 01:82:44,340
where they go and try to go

